he same accounting? Reader: read-item increments d per tag/array. For a list frame decode: tag depth d, payload array d+1, items d+2. Hmm, so reader counts items at d+2 but encoder requires items at d+3?! If encoder is more conservative than decoder, encode at depth 128 might... Test: nested-list-hex 42 decodes fine (reader depth: 42 frames × 3 = 126? Let me compute reader depth for 42 nested lists: outer tag depth 1, its array depth 2, items: inner tag depth 3... per frame +3, innermost empty array: frame 42's array at depth 3×42-1=125? items array at 126? nested-list-hex 42 = 42 tags and arrays + innermost 80. Reader depth of innermost empty array: tag1=1, array1=2, tag2=3, array2=4, ..., tag42=83, array42=84? Wait that's 2 per frame + innermost item... Actually nested-list-hex k = (k-1)×\"d81b 82 <name> 81\" + \"d81b 82 <name> 80\". Per frame: tag(d), array(d+1), name-string(d+2), items-array(d+2). Next frame's tag at d+3. So k frames: innermost items-array at depth 3(k-1)+... = tag_k at 3(k-1)+1, array_k at 3(k-1)+2 = 3k-1. For k=42: 125 ≤ 128 ✓; k=43: 128... 3×43-1 = 128 ✓ accepted? But test says nested-list-hex 43 → :malformed-cbor. Hmm 3×43-1 = 128, deep! rejects when d > 128 → 128 not rejected. But test expects rejection at 43. Let me recount: frame i's tag at depth 3(i-1)+1. k=43: tag43 at 127, array43 at 128, name at 129 → rejected ✓ (name string item is at depth 129 > 128: read-item is called on array elements at inc d = 129 → deep! rejects). OK so reader rejects k=43 due to name at 129. And encoder: list-wire is d, items at d+3: frame i's items (inner list) at... inner list's own tag = d+3 where outer tag is d? items are wired at (+ d 3) — the inner value's outermost item (its tag) sits at d+3. So encoder also consumes 3 per frame ✓. And identifier's name is at d+3 ✓ consistent with reader (name at array d+1's element → d+2? Reader: tag d, array d+1, name d+2). Encoder checks (+ d 3), wires text at d+3 — encoder is one deeper than reader for identifier. Encoder more conservative than decoder would mean some value encodes to bytes that decode rejects? E4: \"both count same CBOR depths so anything encode outputs, decode accepts\". For identifier: encoder requires d+3 ≤ 128; produced bytes: tag(d), array(d+1), name(d+2) → reader needs d+2 ≤ 128. Encoder stricter → anything encoded decodes ✓. Not a soundness issue, just off-by-one conservatism; probably existed since J1 (identifier-wire not Dart specific). Actually is it a problem in the other direction: reader accepts a payload that encode would reject on re-encode? Decode requires re-encode match — if reader accepts an identifier at name depth 128 (tag at 126), re-encode checks tag at d+3 = 129 > 128 → rejects with :unsupported-value instead of returning the value! So there could exist a payload where decode succeeds structurally but fails re-encode with :unsupported-value rather than returning the value. Is that a contract violation? E4 says \"anything encode outputs, decode accepts\" — that direction is fine. The other direction (decode accepts things encode rejects) shouldn't happen for canonical payloads, since canonical payloads are by definition encodable. A payload with a deeply nested identifier at exactly that boundary that decode accepts but re-encode rejects — the rejection class would be :unsupported-value from encode inside decode... decode's contract is to accept exactly canonical payloads; such a payload at the edge of the depth limit — is it canonical? If encode never produces it (encoder rejects), it's not in the canonical set, and rejecting is correct, but the class :unsupported-value instead of :malformed-cbor for decode rejection is weird. Extremely edge-case, existing since J1 (identifier-wire unchanged?), not Dart specific. Could mention as P3. Actually wait — reader depth for identifier: tag at d (deep! d), read-item recurses into content at d+1 (array), array's elements at d+2 (name text, deep! d+2). So max tag depth for reader acceptance = 126. Encoder: identifier at tag depth d requires deep! (+ d 3) ≤ 128 → d ≤ 125. So tag at depth 126: reader accepts, re-encode rejects with :unsupported-value. Payload bytes would have name at depth 128 = allowed by reader. So decode of such a payload throws :unsupported-value. Contract: \"decode accepts exactly one canonical payload\" — canonical means what encode can produce, so rejecting is correct; class mismatch (:unsupported-value during decode, rather than :malformed-cbor) is minor. But is this new in J2? deep! (+ d 3) in identifier-wire — probably from J1. Not a regression; P3 at most. Same question for list-wire: deep! (+ d 2) and items at d+3. Reader: list frame tag d, array d+1, items at d+2, name also at d+2. So reader accepts items at d+2 where encoder puts at d+3? Encoder's deep! (+ d 2) checks... hmm, encoder's items-wire at (+ d 3) but deep! only (+ d 2). Reader for inner item at d+2 → accepts up to tag depth d=128 for inner? Inner tag at reader depth d+2 ≤ 128 → outer d ≤ 126. Encoder: outer frame at d, inner tag wired at d+3, its own deep! at its depth → d+3 ≤ 128 → d ≤ 125. Again off by one, same minor issue. Existing since J1. Test pins nested-list 42/43 passing. Fine — but 42×3=126, reader's innermost array at 125, name at... consistent.\n\nOK, these depth off-by-ones are existing J1 behavior, pinned by tests; not J2 findings beyond noting.\n\n23. **`encode` at depth: `wire-of value 1`** ✓.\n\n24. **`decimal` on Dart stores exponent as int (via .toInt) — decimal-exponent returns int; re-encode int-wire(big e) ✓.\n\n25. **Dart's `hash` function on strings/vecs** — cljd provides ✓.\n\n26. **`(= (count coll) expected)`** ✓.\n\n27. **`constructible?` uses tree-seq coll? seq** — on Dart, `not-constructible` is a keyword `::not-constructible` = :dao.jing.cbor-fixtures/not-constructible. `(some #(= not-constructible %) (tree-seq coll? seq value))` — fine for `host-value \"char\"` returning not-constructible as top-level value.\n\n28. **`host-value \"inst\"` on Dart: DateTime.parse** → encode rejects with :unsupported-value (type-name). wire-of falls to :else → rejected ✓. But `constructible?` — tree-seq over DateTime: coll? is false → checks only itself ✓.\n\n29. **`\"uuid\"` on Dart: `(uuid v)`** — cljd's uuid → rejected with :unsupported-value ✓.\n\n30. **`\"fn\"` identity** — rejected ✓.\n\n31. **`(->Probe 1)` record** — record? on cljd? Does cljd support defrecord? The fixture defines `(defrecord Probe [a])` at line 139 — does cljd compile this? If cljd doesn't support defrecord, this fixture wouldn't compile on Dart... but the lane passes, so it does (ClojureDart supports defrecord? I think cljd supports deftype/defrecord). And `record?` dispatch on cljd — does cljd implement record?? If record? is false for Probe on Dart, then Probe would fall through wire-of dispatch: not map?, not vector?... defrecord is a map → `(map? x)` true → would encode as a map! Unless record? catches first. wire-of checks `(record? x)` before `(map? x)` (lines 1013-1014) ✓. Does cljd have record?? If not, compilation would fail on unresolved symbol → lane wouldn't pass. Since it passes, record? exists and returns true for cljd records (does cljd's defrecord produce a type satisfying clj's record?? Hmm — actually in cljd, defrecord is... I can't verify; tests pass so `host/record` case rejects correctly on Dart. encode-rejection case with record input — is that rejected as :unsupported-value? If record? is false on Dart, map? is true → map-wire of {:a 1} → encodes fine → no rejection → test fails. Lane passes → fine.)\n\n32. **`refusal-of` on Dart catches `Object`** — Dart throws Exception/Error; Object catches all ✓. But note: catching Object in cljd... ex-info is an Exception ✓.\n\n33. **`guarded` catches Object** ✓.\n\n34. **`type-name` on Dart: `.-runtimeType`** ✓.\n\n35. **Now the question of \"Dart `is` failure throws\" in decode runner** — addressed above: not handled in canonical/decode runners; failures still surface but hide later cases. Also: inside `guarded`, the catch calls `(is false ...)` which throws on Dart → propagates → but importantly, doseq interruption means remaining cases don't run; the test as a whole fails (which is correct signal). P3.\n\nActually wait — is it true that `is` throws on Dart? In cljd's clojure.test port, assertion failures do... The implementer's own report says so and restructured one runner accordingly. If `is` throws, then in `refusal-of`... no `is` there. In canonical runner, `is` failures throw inside `testing` inside `guarded`'s f → caught by guarded → `(is false ...)` throws → doseq interrupted → deftest fails. Result: failure is reported (as a thrown exception with the message id \" threw ...\"). Later cases hidden. P3 (diagnostics only).\n\nBut also the final `(is (empty? @unexpected) ...)` in encode-rejection — if that `is` throws on Dart, deftest fails ✓ fine (last statement).\n\n36. **`skip!` uses println** — output on Dart is fine.\n\n37. **`groups-hold-on-produced-bytes`** — runs on Dart ✓ no `is` throw guard... uses is inside doseq; if it fails, it throws and hides rest. Same P3 class.\n\n38. **`encoding-ignores-ambient-print-settings`** — `binding [*print-length* ...]` on cljd — do these vars exist in cljd? cljd's clojure.core has *print-length* etc. binding works ✓ (lane passes).\n\n39. **`payloads-are-one-item-then-end`** — `(re-find #\"d90100|d819\" h)` — regex literal on cljd: does cljd support #\"...\"? ClojureDart supports regex literals (Dart RegExp). re-find on cljd — implemented ✓ (lane passes).\n\n40. **`jing/sha256-bytes`** on Dart — existing (J1? dao.jing probably has cljd half) ✓.\n\nNow **question 2 details**: \"does the package re-encode, reorder, narrow (integer double to int) or allocate differently?\"\n\n- Reordering: CborMap.fromIterables preserves insertion order per docs; adapter sorts. ✓ But `CborMap.fromIterables` — if the keys iterable is a clj vector — does it copy to a Dart map internally? CborMap stores LinkedHashMap of key→value; if two keys are `==` in Dart, they collapse! Keys are CborValue objects — CborValue implements ==? In cbor package, does CborValue subclass implement equality? If CborString(\"a\") == CborString(\"a\") (value equality), duplicate keys would collapse — but duplicates are rejected earlier by hex. Different hex → different bytes → are they == as objects? CborInt(1) vs CborSmallInt(1): hmm, would both be produced for the same integer by the shared code? `integer` chooses based on Dart type. Two keys \"1\" (int) and BigInt 1 (CborInt) — different bytes? CborSmallInt 1 → 0x01; CborInt BigInt.one → also 0x01! Same bytes → hex duplicate check rejects first ✓. So CborMap equality-based collapsing could only lose entries with identical bytes, which are rejected earlier. But wait — is CborInt == CborSmallInt with same value? In package, CborSmallInt extends CborInt; equality compares values → they could be ==. Irrelevant per above.\n\n  But another collapse risk: **CborMap.fromIterables building LinkedHashMap where key equality is by Dart == on CborValue objects.** Two keys with different bytes but equal by ==? E.g., CborString \"a\" vs CborString \"a\" — same bytes. CborBytes vs CborString — different types, not equal. CborInt vs CborFloat? Does the package have CborFloat? Only via tagged frame — float is encoded as a byte string in a frame, never CborFloat. So no.\n\n  Also **CborMap.fromIterables could throw on duplicate keys**? If it detects them? Doesn't matter.\n\n- Narrowing: package never receives a double except through byte strings in frames — float64 frame payload is bytes ✓. CborFloat not used ✓.\n\n- Re-encoding strings: CborString writes UTF-8 of the Dart string. Dart strings with unpaired surrogates write '?' — guarded by check-text before CborString creation. All paths to CborString? `text` (called from wire-of after check-text ✓, and identifier-wire after check-text ✓), and `frame` name (a compile-time symbol ✓). And `tagged`/others none. Decode path: utf8 strict decode → valid string, no lone surrogates possible (strict decoder rejects ED A0 80 etc) ✓. But wait — BOM restoration prepends U+FEFF — fine.\n\n  Hmm, actually one more path: **`String.fromCharCodes` in fixtures for {\"utf16\" codes}** — can create lone surrogate strings for rejection tests; codec rejects in check-text"}}
