nical sort). Set node order: set-wire sorts explicitly. Array order preserved (mapv). ✓.\n\nAnother potential regression: in `members-wire`, key hex computed via `wire/encode kw` — on JVM/Node, this encodes the key node standalone with Boring. That was also true in J1 (needed for duplicate check). Same.\n\nNow, is there anything that could change JVM/Node bytes for out-of-corpus inputs?\n\n- `int-wire`: unchanged logic? Probably same as J1.\n- `float-wire`: same.\n- One thing: `identifier-wire` now uses `wire/array-node` over `[ns-node name-node]` — vector, same as before.\n- `list-wire` uses `wire/frame 'dao.jing/list (mapv ...)` — frame's payload was previously mapv too.\n\nI think JVM/Node is safe. Since I can't diff, I'll state that I reviewed the adapter layer as identity functions and that the corpus + full JVM/Node lanes are reported green.\n\n**Deeper trap hunting on Dart:**\n\n1. `check-text` + `count` on strings in cljd: needs verification. Let me check how cljd handles count on strings. Can I find ClojureDart's source? Outside the repo. Hmm. cljd docs: \"Strings: ClojureDart strings are Dart Strings. count returns the number of UTF-16 code units\" — I think cljd implements String as a seq of chars (1-code-unit strings?) like Clojure JVM. Actually, ClojureDart's `seq` on a String returns a sequence of String (each 1 code unit)? Or runes? If cljd's seq/count were rune-based, astral text cases in the corpus would fail check-text or the text DSL builder `(text ...)` (via `String.fromCharCodes`). The report says all 209 canonical pass on Dart, and the corpus probably includes astral/emoji cases (uni group). Let me quickly grep the corpus for astral cases to confirm coverage exists.\n\n2. **`subs` and `char-code` indices consistent** — both code-unit based in cljd (subs on cljd strings uses code units like JVM).\n\n3. **`span-key`/`bytes-hex`** on Dart uses `.toRadixString ^int b 16` — b is int ✓.\n\n4. **`hex-int`** cljd's reduce over `(count h)` string with `