{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, read the contract document (docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md) — the \"Encoding contract\" and \"Numeric identity\" sections — and then also check the architect's J1 sign-off and the J2 section of the work package. Also, I need to verify the JVM/Node non-regression issue: I need the git diff of boring.cljc. Without shell access, I can't get the old version. However, the J1 committed version is probably in git objects; I can't read it. But I can reason: J1's cbor.cljc used to build the wire directly with boring values (e.g., `(into {} ...)`, vectors, TaggedValue). Currently it goes through adapter functions, which are identity on JVM/Node. Identity functions return exactly the same thing. The only concern: `bool` returns `b` — but in wire-of, `(wire/bool x)` where x is a boolean; identity. `null` returns nil. `text` returns s. `byte-string` returns bs. `integer` returns n. `array-node` returns nodes (a vector — previously mapv produced a vector; still mapv). `map-node` does `(into {} (map ...))` from wired triples — previously, map-wire probably built `(into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [kw vw]) ...))`. Question: does the key order of the map passed to boring affect anything? Boring's :canonical sorts keys, so it doesn't.\n\nOne subtle change: members-wire now computes `wire/encode` of each key node for hex-sorting purposes — this probably also existed in J1 (map keys are sorted by canonical byte order for duplicate detection). Actually, in J1's map, since Boring sorts the keys, the encode side only needed duplicate detection by canonical bytes. Same now. But wait — `map-node` on JVM/Node builds a Clojure map with key node → value node; duplicate detection is done before construction via `wired`'s hex. No problem.\n\nPotential regression path: `set-wire` sorts with `(sort-by first wired)`, where first is the hex string; the result is passed to `wire/tagged 258 (mapv second ...)`. Previously probably the same. `sort-by` on hex strings — stable sort, lexicographic ordering on lowercase hex: does lexicographic ordering on hex strings match byte-wise canonical ordering? In CBOR's canonical map key ordering (RFC 8949 core deterministic: byte-wise lexicographic on the encoded keys), shorter prefix comes first. Lowercase hex string comparison: \"0a\" vs \"0a01\": \"0a\" is a prefix and shorter → comes first. Byte-wise: [0x0a] vs [0x0a, 0x01] → shorter prefix first. Same. Hex characters: '0'-'9' < 'a'-'f' in ASCII, and byte value ordering matches nibble ordering. So no problem. This is also used in cljd.cljd's map-node.\n\nNow, main review points to verify by reading the code:\n\n1. **Correctness of Dart writer.**\n   - `integer`: uses CborSmallInt for Dart's int, CborInt for BigInt. Question: does `cbor` 6.5.1's CborSmallInt write the shortest head including 8-byte? CborSmallInt takes an `int`; for values beyond the signed 64-bit range... Dart's int is signed 64-bit. b->int on Dart: `(.toInt b)` if `isValidInt` — Dart's BigInt.isValidInt is true when it fits in a signed 64-bit int. So integers in [-2^63, 2^63-1] are Dart int; values in [2^63, 2^64-1] and [-2^64, -2^63-1] are BigInt → CborInt. Does CborInt handle negative BigInts? In cbor package 6.x, CborInt covers both major types 0 and 1, accepting BigInt. Probably fine. Can I read the package source if it's vendored? It's in the pub cache, not in the repo. Check if there's a pubspec/deps directory in the repo... The report says the package source is outside allowed directories. Can't verify package internals; I'll rely on the report's probe claims and note residual risks.\n   \n   - Actually wait: does `cborEncode` for CborSmallInt with a negative int write major type 1 correctly? Standard. Shortest head: the package should write minimal representation. The implementer probed it. But one subtle trap: CborSmallInt for values ≥ 2^63 can't exist as Dart int. Fine.\n   \n   - `text`: `CborString. s` — does the package write minimal UTF-8? CborString stores the string and encodes as UTF-8, major type 3, with length = UTF-8 byte length. Should. But: does the package write the string as-is, or re-encode surrogates? The codec checks surrogates first in check-text. But wait — where is check-text applied? In wire-of for strings, identifier-wire for ns/name. On decode, `utf8` strictly decodes; then re-encode. OK.\n   \n   - Bigger trap: CborString with `.type` default is definite. Yes.\n   \n   - `byte-string`: CborBytes over Uint8List — writes major type 2 with byte count. OK.\n   \n   - `tagged` for byte content: `CborBytes. content .tags [tag]` — writes the tag head then the byte string. Tags 2/3 with minimal magnitude bytes: magnitude-bytes returns a minimal big-endian Uint8List; for zero → empty list, tag 2 over empty byte string (0xc240) — canonical for bignum 0? Actually, zero in a corpus shouldn't use bignum (it's in 64-bit range). But re-encoding for decode: tag 2/3 payload comes from the wire, and the canonicality check requires re-encode to match; if input had non-minimal magnitude bytes, re-encode differs → rejected as non-canonical. Fine.\n   \n   - `frame`: tag 27 over CborList [CborString name, content]. OK.\n   \n   - `array-node`: CborList with .type definite. Does CborList accept a Clojure vector via `(vec nodes)`? In cljd, `vec` produces a CLJ vector implementing List? `(cbor/CborList. (vec nodes) ...)` — CborList's constructor expects a Dart List. ClojureDart vectors... hmm. ClojureDart's clj vector is a Dart object but does it implement Dart's `List` interface? I think ClojureDart collections implement Dart interfaces (clj vectors implement List). Actually yes: ClojureDart maps implement Dart Map and vectors implement List for interop. I recall cljd vectors satisfy `is List`. OK.\n   \n   - `map-node`: CborMap.fromIterables with Clojure vectors of keys and values (mapv). Fine. Keys sorted by hex. But duplicate keys impossible (checked earlier). Note: CborMap.fromIterables — does the package dedupe or reorder? Map keys here are cbor objects (CborString, CborInt, etc.). If two distinct wire nodes are `==` as Dart objects, fromIterables might collapse them? Keys are already duplicate-checked by hex, so distinct bytes; but could distinct nodes compare equal via Dart equality? E.g., CborInt 1 and CborSmallInt 1? Both would encode to the same bytes, so hex duplicate check catches them. What about CborString(\"a\") vs... same bytes → caught. So no.\n   \n   - `encode`: `Uint8List.fromList (cborEncode wire)` — does cborEncode return Uint8List? In cbor 6.x, `cborEncode(value)` returns `Uint8List`. Actually is it the cbor package's top-level function `cborEncode`? Package API: I think `cborEncode(CborValue value)` exists as `Uint8List cborEncode(CborValue value)` in cbor 6.x. Yes (added around 6.0 as a replacement for cbor.encode? there's a `cborEncode` function). OK.\n   \n   - One real question: **tag encoding with CborBytes `.tags [tag]`** — does the package write the tag head first? Yes, tags are written before the item.\n   \n   - **CborInt vs CborSmallInt**: in cbor 6.5.1, CborSmallInt is a subtype of CborInt for values that fit in int. `CborInt. n` where n is BigInt. Fine.\n   \n   - Potential trap: `CborString` writes text with length in bytes — but if the string contains non-BMP characters (surrogate pairs), UTF-8 encoding is 4 bytes; fine.\n\n2. **Shared reader Dart host traps:**\n   - `read-head` for ai 24-26: `(+ (* acc 256) (byte-at bs i))` — Dart int is 64-bit, max 4-byte arg is 2^32-1, fine.\n   - `byte-at` on Dart uses `(. bs \"[]\" i)`, Uint8List → returns int 0-255. Fine.\n   - ai 27 `span-big`: builds BigInt from bytes. Fine.\n   - `length-of`: rejects on Dart if BigInt exceeds max-length; `.toInt` OK. But note: on Dart, `host-big?` checks `dart/is? x BigInt`. read-head's `(< ai 26)` path returns a Dart int → not BigInt → fine.\n   - But: **Dart int overflow in span-key / bytes-hex?** None.\n   - `read-item` major type 7, ai 27 → `:float` item — then `item->value :float` rejects :native-float. Note that float items of ai 25/26 also get `:float` kind `(item :float ai pos)` — for ai 25/26/27, the kind is `:float` and value is ai... Wait, looking: `(= ai 24)` is simple; `:else` → `(item :float ai pos)`. ai ∈ {25,26,27} → :float. item->value rejects :native-float. Good — but ai 25/26 half/single floats: canonical rejection class should be :native-float per corpus. OK, no behavior change.\n   \n   - **BOM handling in Dart's `utf8` function**: the loop strips BOM sequences (0xef 0xbb 0xbf) and prepends U+FEFF characters. Condition `(<= (+ at 3) end)` — fine. Then decode the rest with Utf8Decoder. Is Utf8Decoder strict by default? dart:convert's Utf8Decoder: default constructor `Utf8Decoder({this.allowMalformed = false})` — strict, throws FormatException on overlong/surrogates/out-of-range. Good.\n   \n   Wait — is it true that Dart's utf8.decode strips the BOM? Actually yes: Dart's `Utf8Decoder` strips a leading BOM (documented behavior: \"decoding strips a leading byte-order mark\"). The fix restores it. But subtlety: the fix prepends `boms` U+FEFF characters — but does Dart's decoder strip only one BOM or multiple? Documented: strips a leading BOM (one). If input has two BOMs, decoder strips the first, leaves the second as content? Hmm — actually the decoder strips one leading BOM; the second BOM remains as U+FEFF content. The loop strips all leading BOMs and re-prepends all of them, and decoding starts after them, so it's correct either way.\n   \n   Another subtlety: **indefinite-length text chunks** (lines 1149-1177): each chunk is decoded with `utf8`; on Dart, a chunk beginning with BOM bytes gets BOM handling — correct. Chunks cannot split a BOM mid-sequence... A chunk could end with 0xef and the next start with 0xbb 0xbf — in that case, chunk 1's decoding of 0xef fails as invalid UTF-8 → rejected. That's the correct canonical behavior anyway (indefinite text is not in profile; profile only accepts definite? Actually, profile — does decode accept indefinite-length text at all? re-encode check: encode always writes definite, so indefinite input fails re-encode → non-canonical. But strict UTF-8 is also checked first. Fine.)\n   \n   - **`check-text` on Dart**: uses `.codeUnitAt` — Dart strings are UTF-16 code units, so unpaired surrogate detection works the same as JS. Good. But wait — `count` on a string in ClojureDart: does `(count s)` return the number of code units or runes? In ClojureDart, `count` on a String is... cljd's string implementation: `clj.core/count` on a string — I think ClojureDart treats strings as CharSequence with code-unit semantics (like Clojure JVM's .length? no — JVM's count is the number of UTF-16 code units via String.length). In ClojureDart, does String implement clj's ISeqable? Hmm. ClojureDart docs: strings are seqable by code unit? Recalling cljd's `count` on a String... In ClojureDart, `String` is given clj protocols; `seq` of a string yields single-character strings? Actually, in ClojureDart `seq` on a String yields chars? Dart has no char type. ClojureDart's `count` on a String returns length in code units (String.length). I think cljd defines count on strings as `.length` (code units), consistent with JVM. And `subs` also uses code-unit indices. If count returned rune count and char-code used code-unit indices, check-text could be inconsistent for astral characters. Risky to assert without checking cljd source. Since surrogate pairs are 2 code units and also counted as 2 (rune = 1 for pair). If count is rune-based, for \"a\\uD83D\\uDE00\", n would be 2 (rune count), and char-code at i=1 would be codeUnitAt(1) = 0xD83D high surrogate, check i+1=2 < n=2? no → rejected as unpaired surrogate — false rejection of valid text! That would be a P1 if count were rune-based. But the corpus probably has astral test cases (uni/*). The report says the canonical corpus passes on Dart — if the corpus has emoji/astral text cases and the full cljd lane passes, then count must be code-unit based. Corpus case \"uni/astral\" probably exists. Let me check corpus for astral cases. Also `subs`/`apply str` etc.\n   \n   Actually, ClojureDart: strings are represented as Dart Strings; cljd implements `-count` on String as `.length` (UTF-16 code units) — fairly confident since it aims for JVM semantics.\n   \n   - **`b->int`**: Dart's `.isValidInt` / `.toInt`. For values that fit in signed 64-bit → int. Encoding `integer` uses CborSmallInt for int. Note: Dart int is signed 64-bit; values in [-2^64, -2^63-1] are BigInt → CborInt. Good.\n   \n   - **`magnitude-bytes`** on Dart: loops over bytes, `(Uint8List.fromList (vec acc))` — acc is a list of ints built by prepending (conj to () list) — wait, `conj` to a Clojure list prepends. Loop: acc starts as (), each iteration prepends the low byte, then b >>= 8. So acc is little-endian → reversed? Let's trace: b = 0x0102. Iteration 1: prepend (b & 0xff)=0x02 → acc=(0x02); b=0x01. Iteration 2: prepend 0x01 → acc=(0x01 0x02). Result list order: (0x01, 0x02) — big-endian. Correct.\n   \n   - **`span-big`** uses `(big (byte-at bs i))` → on Dart `(big int)` → BigInt.from. Fine.\n   \n   - **`float-fields` on Dart**: `(>= hi 0x80000000)` — Dart's getUint32 returns int; comparison is fine. `(bit-shift-right hi 20)` — hi is non-negative (uint32), fine.\n   \n   - **`float-wire` on Dart**: ByteData big-endian by default (Endian.big is default). setFloat64 → getUint8List... `.asUint8List (.-buffer bd)` — full 8 bytes. NaN → canonical 7ff8000000000000. Good. But note: on Dart, does `setFloat64` for NaN preserve payload bits? For non-NaN, writes f directly; NaN case is handled. But: Dart VM canonicalizes NaN — `.-isNaN ^double f` then writes fixed bits. Good.\n   \n   - **Signed zero on Dart**: `(.-isNaN)` is false; setFloat64 of -0.0 writes 8000000000000000. Dart preserves -0.0 as double. And `float64?` on Dart: `dart/is? x double` — -0.0 is double, so it takes the float path, not host-integer (dart/is? -0.0 int is false). Good. But `host-integer?` on Dart: `dart/is? x int` — Dart double 1.0 is not int, good (kind preservation).\n   \n   - **`(cbor/float64 x)`**: `.toDouble ^num` — works for int→double, and for double. Note: `float64 1` on Dart gives double 1.0. Fine.\n   \n   - **Decimal carrier hash on Dart**: `(hash [exponent (str mantissa)])` — exponent is int, mantissa is BigInt; str of BigInt. Fine.\n   \n   - **Dart's Rational -equiv/-hash**: `(hash [(str numerator) (str denominator)])`. Fine.\n   \n   - **Equiv-hash on Dart**: numeric? uses host-integer? etc. Byte payload: `wire/byte-payload?` → cljd version. Fine.\n   \n   - **`collapse?`**: group-by equiv-hash — hash values within a host; fine.\n   \n   - **Set collapse check on Dart**: members-wire checks duplicates and collapse by hex of `wire/encode kw`. In Dart, wire/encode produces Uint8List; bytes-hex via span-key. Fine.\n   \n   - **`built`**: count check after `(into {} pairs)` and `(into #{}`. On Dart, `into #{}` uses clj's set constructor — does cljd's set use Dart's equality on elements? For double values 0.0 and -0.0: cljd's `#{}` — does it merge 0.0 and -0.0? Report says \"0.0 and -0.0 merge in a set\" — but wait, that's for Dart's Set<double>. Does ClojureDart's `#{}` use clj hash/equiv? The report says the set constructor merged (so the coll/set-signed-zero-collapse skip). Hmm, actually the skip is about the fixture's input builder `(reduce conj #{} items)` merging. And the decode path's `built` catches merges via host-collapse rejection. The report says host-collapse never fires on Dart — but if -0.0/0.0 merge in a cljd set, decoding a canonical set containing both would... but such a set can't be canonical (equivalent members rejected). OK.\n   \n   However, `into #{}` where a double and an int of the same value exist? `(= 1 1.0)` is true on Dart; the report says \"the set still holds both values\". So cljd's set distinguishes int 1 and double 1.0 (different runtime types → probably different hashes). OK.\n   \n   - **Keyword/symbol equality with slashes on Dart**: report says they're compared by field, and both are held. The test `slash-crossed-symbol-set-is-held-or-refused-never-merged` expects ::none on cljd. Good.\n   \n   - **`keyword` with ns on Dart**: `(keyword ns name)` — cljd's keyword. Decode re-encode: identifier-wire uses namespace/name fields. Fine.\n   \n   - **Lists on Dart have metadata from the constructor**: frame->value uses `(with-meta (apply list ...) nil)`. Also `fresh-list` in fixtures. Encode path: `(meta x)` on a cljd list with :tag metadata → meta-wire would emit a with-meta frame with {:tag Type} → wire-of on a Dart Type → rejected as :unsupported-value. The fresh-list helper clears it. But in `nested-list` test, cljd uses `(with-meta (list acc) nil)`. And `list-wire` for a decoded list — frame->value clears meta. OK. But: are there other paths on Dart where a list is constructed with constructor metadata and encoded? E.g., `\"seq\"` case: `(build (lazy-seq (seq (items))))` — `seq` on a vector in cljd — does the resulting seq have metadata? Probably nil. `lazy-seq` — probably no metadata. Also `members-wire` uses `mapv`, `seq m`, etc. — internal nodes, not encoded values. Fine.\n   \n   Actually wait — meta-wire on a list with :tag metadata: `(meta x)` returns {:tag ...}, and it would try to encode. In fixtures, fresh-list clears it. Fine.\n\n3. **JVM/Node regression issue**: adapters are identity. One change: `map-node` now receives `wired` triples and builds `(into {} (map (fn [[_ kw vw]] [kw vw])) wired)`. Previously J1 probably built the map from pairs directly. Behavior identical. `array-node` is identity on a vector. `integer` is identity — b->int returns long or BigInt on JVM, number or BigInt on JS. Boring previously received the same. `null` → nil; previously the code passed nil directly. `bool` → b; `(wire/bool x)` where x is truthy/falsy? In wire-of, `(boolean? x)` is checked first, so x is exactly true/false. Identity.\n\n   One subtlety: **`text`** is identity — check-text returns s. Same as J1.\n   \n   **`byte-string`**: `(wire/byte-string (copy-range x 0 (blen x)))` — fresh copy; J1 probably also copied (the byte-isolation test pins this). Fine.\n\n   `frame` and `tagged` unchanged (not adapters — they still build TaggedValue/tagged-literal on JVM/Node, cljd builds package objects).\n\n   Can bytes change on JVM/Node? Since adapters are identity and call sites are equivalent, no. But one potential difference: in J1, `set-wire` might have passed sorted nodes; same now. And `identifier-wire` uses `wire/array-node` over a vector of two nodes — previously probably a plain vector literal. Same bytes.\n\n   Hmm, but I can't see the actual J1 code to diff. Without git access, I should note this as a constraint, and verify via the J1 sign-off document's description or committed version... Can I read the committed version via git objects? Not without shell. There might be a way: read_file on .git files — packed objects are zlib; can't decompress. So I'll note the limitation, but the fact that the corpus test passes on JVM/Node (32 tests, 4447 assertions; node 1607 tests) plus the adapters being identity gives reasonable confidence. The report says JVM and Node lanes are green after changes — corpus pins bytes for 209 canonical cases.\n\n   Potential regression outside the corpus: adapter `integer`'s docstring says \"host integer in [-2^64, 2^64) range\" — identity, no narrowing. Fine.\n\n4. **Ratified constraints**: decimal window [-2147483647, 2147483648] — code lines 537-545 ✓. max-depth 128 ✓ (lines 850-858). float64 kind preservation on Dart: `dart/is? x double` ✓; no narrowing: host-integer? excludes double ✓. BigInt for exact integers: b->int ✓. Explicit outer with-meta frame: meta-wire uses wire/frame 'clojure/with-meta ✓ (on Dart, frame writes tag 27). Structural validation before package instantiation: decode doesn't use package at all ✓. Strict re-encode after decode ✓ (lines 1384-1385). host-collapse only if Dart merges — `built` shared ✓.\n\n5. **Tests**: skips with reasons by id ✓ (skip-by-id). Encode rejection runner collects host merges and asserts at end ✓ (lines 118, 137, 142-143, 148-149). But decode-refusal runner (lines 152-159) and canonical runner (lines 95-111): on Dart, `is` failure throws. Both wrap each case with `guarded` catching Object and reporting `(is false ...)` — but on Dart, an `is` inside `guarded` throws and gets caught by guarded, which then calls `(is false ...)` again... which throws again, interrupting doseq? Wait: `guarded` catches exceptions from f and calls `(is false msg)`. If `is` throws on Dart, the `(is false ...)` inside the catch also throws, and it propagates out of `guarded`, interrupting the doseq loop — hiding later cases. Hmm. How does clojure.test work on ClojureDart? In cljd, do `is` failures throw? The report says \"on Dart, `is` failures throw\". clojure.test on cljd: assertion failures are recorded and... Actually in cljd's test runner, `is` failure throws an exception (because cljd's clojure.test implementation reports via throw?). The report explicitly says \"on Dart, `is` failures throw and hide later cases\", which is why the encode-rejection runner collects and asserts once at end. But the canonical runner and decode-rejection runner still use `is` per case within `guarded`/doseq. If `is` throws on failure in those runners, the throw propagates: in canonical runner, `is` is inside the `#(testing id ...)` wrapped by `guarded`. guarded catches and calls `(is false ...)` which throws again → doseq interrupted, later cases skipped, but the test as a whole fails (throw propagates to deftest → test failure). So failures are still detected but hide later cases — degrading diagnostics, not losing failures. In encode-rejection, the fix was needed because... actually the same concern? The difference: in encode-rejection, `(is ...)` in the catch is — hmm.\n\n   Actually wait, why did the implementer only fix the encode-rejection runner? The report says \"encode-rejection runner now collects unexpected host merges and asserts once at end, because on Dart a failing `is` throws and hides later cases\". The same structure exists in the canonical and decode runners: `(is ...)` failures inside guarded → guarded catches → `(is false ...)` throws again → loop interrupted. So a single failure in canonical or decode on Dart hides later cases. Failure still surfaces (deftest fails), so not a soundness hole for pass/fail judgment, but a diagnostics gap. Review question 5 asks: \"Dart `is` failure throws and can hide later cases (is that handled everywhere including the decode runner?)\". Answer: no — canonical and decode runners still have `is` inside `guarded` loops; a first failure interrupts the rest of that deftest. Minor (P3) since the failure itself is reported; also `guarded`'s re-throwing `is false` means the original exception message gets replaced... Actually guarded catches e and reports `(is false (str id \" threw \" ...))` — if that `is` throws, doseq interrupts with that throw; test framework reports the failure. Remaining cases untested → could hide additional failures but never turns a failure into a pass. P3.\n\n   Also the `slash-crossed-symbol-set-is-held-or-refused-never-merged` test expects `::none` on cljd — meaning decode succeeds. But decode success requires re-encode match and set retains both symbols. Report says Dart retains both. ✓.\n\n   Dart's `collapse-not-applicable`: signed-zero and vector-list — matches report. But wait: JVM includes `coll/map-integer-width-duplicate` and `coll/set-decimal-scale-collapse`; Dart includes neither → on Dart, decimal-scale collapse must be live/rejected. The report says \"the other 7 collapse and duplicate cases (integer-width, both NaNs, int/float, float/decimal, int/ratio, decimal scale) hold both members on Dart\". integer-width: on Dart, `coll/map-integer-width-duplicate` — is it about map keys 1 (small int) and 1 (BigInt)? On Dart, `#{1 (BigInt.parse \"1\")}`? Map `(into {})` with keys 1 and BigInt 1 — are these different in clj maps? Dart's BigInt(1) == int 1? In Dart, `BigInt.one == 1` is false (different types; BigInt's operator== requires BigInt). And the report says integer-width stays separate on Dart → live, rejected. And E1's proposed Dart column: \"signed-zero and vector-list N/A (merged); all others live\". Cross-check with code's `collapse-not-applicable :dart` = those two ✓.\n\n   Hmm wait, but the signed-zero merge: on Dart, does `#{0.0 -0.0}` merge? Does cljd's set use Dart equality on doubles? Actually, in clj semantics, set uses hash and equiv. Dart's 0.0 and -0.0: `0.0 == -0.0` is true and same hashCode → merges. ✓ consistent.\n\n   But there's a subtle inconsistency: on Dart, `(cbor/float64 0)` and `(cbor/float64 -0.0)` are both plain doubles — and Float64 carrier is cljs-only. The set merges them. But decode's `built` check: if a canonical set payload had both 0.0 and -0.0... it can't be canonical (equivalent → rejected). Fine.\n\n   Now errata E1's proposed Dart column — the task asks me to validate against code: code's :dart collapse-not-applicable = {signed-zero, vector-list}; all other E1 rows must be live on Dart per code (not in skip set, and test asserts rejection). ✓ consistent with implementer's proposal. But errata itself doesn't have a Dart column yet — orchestrator action needed. Not a code defect; note as P3/gap.\n\n6. **Safety**: no edits to dao.jing, backends, etc. — git status shows only the four modified files and new cljd file (and collab prompt). Frozen files unmodified (git status doesn't list them). print/parse: report claims none; let me grep for `print` usage — test's `println` inside skip! (allowed? \"no print/parse\" probably refers to codec doing bytes via print/read). In codec files: no print in cbor.cljc. parse: BigInt.parse in fixtures and codecs 