ets metadata to nil (or empty?). On JVM, with-meta nil gives meta nil ✓. On cljd, meta becomes nil, and meta-wire sees nil → no frame ✓.\n\n22. **`\"seq\"` DSL**: `(build (lazy-seq (seq (items))))` — build applies with-meta when there's metadata; lazy-seq on cljd — meta nil ✓. Encode path: `seq?` → list-wire. But wait — `wire-of` checks `(or (list? x) (seq? x))` — LazySeq ✓.\n\nHmm, but `list-wire` docstring says \"(tag, [name items])\" items is d+3, deep! is (+ d 2). Frame is tag (d) → array (d+1) → items (d+2)? Let me re-check depth accounting: list frame = tag 27 over [name, items-array]. Items at depth d: tag d, its array content d+1, array's elements d+2. But code says deep! (+ d 2) and items are (+ d 3). Off by one? For identifier-wire: deep! (+ d 3), components are at d+3: tag d, array d+1, elements d+2. Code uses d+3 — more conservative than needed? Hmm, E4 says list frames consume 3 levels: tag(1), array(2), items(3). So the tag itself is d, array is d+1, items are d+2... \"consume 3 levels\" means items are at d+2 relative to frame's d? Nested-list test: k lists → CBOR depth 3k. For k=1: tag(d1), array(d2), inner empty array(d3)? \"dao.jing/list\" frame payload: [name-string, items-array]. Top tag depth 1; its content array depth 2; items inside that array depth 3. Yes — items at d+2 when frame is d... but code wires items at (+ d 3). Hmm: list-wire is called with x at depth d (tag is d). Payload array at d+1, items inside array at d+2. But code: `(deep! (+ d 2) ...)` then items are `(+ d 3)`. This counts one extra level. Wait — deep! (+ d 2) checks that d+2 ≤ 128 for... tag is d, array is d+1, so deep! checks array is d+1? They check +2 and items at +3. So one level conservative. But the test pins: nested-list 42 → depth 126 = 3×42 ✓, 43 → 129 rejected ✓. So the depth model matches E4 (3 per frame) — meaning per frame levels consumed = 3, so items are conceptually at d+3, where d is the *next* frame's tag... whatever — tests pin behavior and pass on all h