a vector, vec is ~identity. clj vector as Dart List: does ClojureDart's vector implement Dart's List interface? I think yes (cljd makes its collections interop with Dart: PersistentVector implements List). Hmm, actually recalling ClojureDart's docs on interop: clj collections can be passed where Dart List/Map/Set are expected? cljd.core defines protocols so clj vectors are Dart Lists (`cljd PersistentVector implements List`). I think so — cljd types implement Dart collection interfaces for interop. And `(cbor/CborList. ...)`'s constructor expects `List<dynamic>`? CborList.from? CborList's default constructor takes a List. If clj vectors weren't Dart Lists, this would fail at runtime — but the full Dart lane passes (1568 tests), so it works. ✓.\n\n**FF. `mapv second sorted` → CborMap.fromIterables(keys, values)**: keys is a clj vector as Iterable ✓.\n\n**GG. CborMap.fromIterables with `.type` definite — named argument ✓.\n\n**HH. What about `bool`: `(cbor/CborBool. (true? b))` — normalizes truthy to strict bool ✓ (b is already true/false).\n\n**II. `null` → CborNull. writes 0xf6 ✓.\n\nNow **encoding floats via tag 27 frame `dao.jing/float64`** — `frame`'s name string: `(str tag-symbol)` — on Dart, does str of symbol 'dao.jing/float64 give \"dao.jing/float64\"? cljd's symbol: `(str 'dao.jing/float64)` — hmm, symbol literals in cljd... `str` calls toString → \"dao.jing/float64\" ✓. On JVM/Node, frame uses tagged-literal (unchanged from J1).\n\n**JJ. Metadata frame on Dart**: `wire/frame 'clojure/with-meta [(map-wire kept ...) (build ...)]` — frame's payload is a vector of nodes → content-node → array-node ✓.\n\n**KK. `meta-wire` strips position keys; validated via wire-of before stripping ✓ (line 970-972).\n\nNow the **non-regression** question for JVM/Node paths involving `wire/null` etc. — all identity, so bytes are unchanged. One risk: previously, did the code build a map with `(zipmap keys vals)`, and now `(into {} ...)` — map ordering is irrelevant to Boring (canonical sort). Set node order: set-wire sorts explicitly. Array order preserved (mapv). ✓.\n\nAnother potential regression: in `members-wire`, key hex computed via `wire/encode kw` — on JVM/Node, this encodes the key node standalone with Boring. That was also true in J1 (needed for duplicate check). Same.\n\nNow, is there anything that could change JVM/Node bytes for out-of-corpus inputs?\n\n- `int-wire`: unchanged logic? Probably same as J1.\n- `float-wire`: same.\n- One thing: `identifier-wire` now uses `wire/array-node` over `[ns-node name-node]` — vector, same as before.\n- `list-wire` uses `wire/frame 'dao.jing/list (mapv ...)` — frame's payload was previously mapv too.\n\nI think JVM/Node is safe. Since I can't diff, I'll state that I reviewed the adapter layer as identity functions and that the corpus + full JVM/Node lanes are reported green.\n\n**Deeper trap hunting on Dart:**\n\n1. `check-text` + `count` on strings in cljd: needs verification. Let me check how cljd handles count on strings. Can I find ClojureDart's source? Outside the repo. Hmm. cljd docs: \"Strings: ClojureDart strings are Dart Strings. count returns the number of UTF-16 code units\" — I think cljd implements String as a seq of chars (1-code-unit strings?) like Clojure JVM. Actually, ClojureDart's `seq` on a String returns a sequence of String (each 1 code unit)? Or runes? If cljd's seq/count were rune-based, astral text cases in the corpus would fail check-text or the text DSL builder `(text ...)` (via `String.fromCharCodes`). The report says all 209 canonical pass on Dart, and the corpus probably includes astral/emoji cases (uni group). Let me quickly grep the corpus for astral cases to confirm coverage exists.\n\n2. **`subs` and `char-code` indices consistent** — both code-unit based in cljd (subs on cljd strings uses code units like JVM).\n\n3. **`span-key`/`bytes-hex`** on Dart uses `.toRadixString ^int b 16` — b is int ✓.\n\n4. **`hex-int`** cljd's reduce over `(count h)` string with `subs` ✓.\n\n5. **`hash` of `[(str numerator) (str denominator)]`** — Rational on Dart; but note that Rational on JVM hashes `(hash [numerator denominator])` with BigInteger, and on Dart uses strings — hash values are host-local, fine.\n\n6. **`equiv-hash` of sets/maps**: `(hash (set (map ...)))` — order-independent ✓.\n\n7. **`group-by equiv-hash`** — fine.\n\n8. **`collapse?` uses `equiv`** — on Dart, equiv for numbers uses num= ✓ symmetric.\n\n9. **`members-wire` for maps: `(seq m)` pairs `[k v]`** ✓.\n\n10. **`wire-of` dispatch order on Dart**: `(numeric? x)` before record/map etc. On Dart, `host-integer?` on double → false ✓; but what about `num` type — Dart's int and double are both `num`. `float64? x` → dart/is? x double ✓.\n\n11. **Trap: `dart/is? x int` for a value that's both?** Dart's double is never int (VM), but in compiled JS it would be — cljd compiles to native so fine.\n\n12. **`b->int`'s `.toInt`** on BigInt — for values that fit in signed 64-bit ✓. But wait: Dart's `isValidInt` → \"whether this BigInt fits in an int\" ✓.\n\n13. **`length-of` on Dart**: `(.toInt ^BigInt arg)` after max-length check — but arg could be up to 2^63-1 and fit in int ✓; max-length is 2^53-1 so toInt is safe ✓.\n\n14. **`read-head`'s ai 24-26 reduce with `(+ (* acc 256) ...)`** — Dart int is 64-bit, fine for ≤4 bytes (max 2^32-1) ✓. But on JVM, same code path yields Long ✓ (no change).\n\n15. **`deep!` uses `>` on ints ✓.\n\n16. **`item :tag [(when-not (host-big? arg) arg) inner arg]`** — on Dart, arg for ai<27 is int; for ai 27 it's BigInt → tag nil. But note: `case tag` where tag values like 2, 3 are Dart ints ✓. But `(when-not (host-big? arg) arg)` — on Dart, ai 24-26 gives int arg; fine.\n\n17. **`(case tag ... (refuse :unknown-tag (str \"tag \" raw)))`** — raw is original arg; str of BigInt on Dart is fine ✓.\n\n18. **`item->value` for tag 2/3**: `(span-big (:value p) 0 (blen ...))` — p's :value is Uint8List ✓. Then `(b->int ...)` → int or BigInt ✓. Non-minimal magnitude → re-encode mismatch → non-canonical ✓. Empty byte string with tag 2 → span-big of empty → zero → b->int → 0 → re-encode gives int 0 (not tag 2) → mismatch → non-canonical ✓ (corpus pins).\n\n19. **Set decode `(into #{} values)`** — `into #{}` on Dart with doubles 0.0/-0.0 merging — but equivalent values are rejected earlier by check-members ✓. Different types int 1 vs double 1.0 → equivalent → rejected by collapse ✓. NaN vs NaN: equiv's num= treats canonical NaNs as equal → collapse rejected ✓. But: two different NaN payloads decode to float64-from-bits → both become Dart NaN doubles (payload lost!) → equivalent → collapse? For canonical set, duplicates are rejected by raw duplicate check first anyway. For NaN payload duplicate case (map/set-nan-payload-duplicate), raw bytes differ but values are equivalent → :equality-collapse ✓ on Dart (E1 says live on Dart per proposal). But wait — Dart NaN payload loss: does `float64-from-bits \"7ff8000000000001\"` on Dart return a double whose bits are still ...0001? getFloat64 returns a double; when we later do float-fields(setFloat64) — round trip through ByteData on same VM: Dart VM might normalize NaN when storing to a double variable? On x86/ARM64 NaN payloads generally survive in registers/memory; Dart doesn't canonicalize explicitly. But float-wire special-cases isNaN → writes canonical bits anyway. So re-encode of decoded NaN always yields canonical NaN bits ✓. And canonical corpus NaN cases probably have canonical payload ✓.\n\nHmm — but wait: **does the profile even accept non-canonical NaN payloads on decode?** Re-encode normalizes NaN to 7ff8..., so input with payload ≠ canonical fails re-encode → non-canonical rejection. Corpus decode-rejection probably includes that. ✓ shared code, no Dart specificity.\n\n20. **`(apply list (map ...))` on cljd with metadata** — handled via with-meta nil ✓.\n\n21. **`fresh-list`**: `(with-meta (apply list items) nil)` — does cljd's with-meta nil clear? `with-meta coll nil` — s