osts; consistent since reader uses the same accounting? Reader: read-item increments d per tag/array. For a list frame decode: tag depth d, payload array d+1, items d+2. Hmm, so reader counts items at d+2 but encoder requires items at d+3?! If encoder is more conservative than decoder, encode at depth 128 might... Test: nested-list-hex 42 decodes fine (reader depth: 42 frames × 3 = 126? Let me compute reader depth for 42 nested lists: outer tag depth 1, its array depth 2, items: inner tag depth 3... per frame +3, innermost empty array: frame 42's array at depth 3×42-1=125? items array at 126? nested-list-hex 42 = 42 tags and arrays + innermost 80. Reader depth of innermost empty array: tag1=1, array1=2, tag2=3, array2=4, ..., tag42=83, array42=84? Wait that's 2 per frame + innermost item... Actually nested-list-hex k = (k-1)×\"d81b 82 <name> 81\" + \"d81b 82 <name> 80\". Per frame: tag(d), array(d+1), name-string(d+2), items-array(d+2). Next frame's tag at d+3. So k frames: innermost items-array at depth 3(k-1)+... = tag_k at 3(k-1)+1, array_k at 3(k-1)+2 = 3k-1. For k=42: 125 ≤ 128 ✓; k=43: 128... 3×43-1 = 128 ✓ accepted? But test says nested-list-hex 43 → :malformed-cbor. Hmm 3×43-1 = 128, deep! rejects when d > 128 → 128 not rejected. But test expects rejection at 43. Let me recount: frame i's tag at depth 3(i-1)+1. k=43: tag43 at 127, array43 at 128, name at 129 → rejected ✓ (name string item is at depth 129 > 128: read-item is called on array elements at inc d = 129 → deep! rejects). OK so reader rejects k=43 due to name at 129. And encoder: list-wire is d, items at d+3: frame i's items (inner list) at... inner list's own tag = d+3 where outer tag is d? items are wired at (+ d 3) — the inner value's outermost item (its tag) sits at d+3. So encoder also consumes 3 per frame ✓. And identifier's name is at d+3 ✓ consistent with reader (name at array d+1's element → d+2? Reader: tag d, array d+1, name d+2). Encoder checks (+ d 3), wires text at d+3 — encoder is one