ets metadata to nil (or empty?). On JVM, with-meta nil gives meta nil ✓. On cljd, meta becomes nil, and meta-wire sees nil → no frame ✓.\n\n22. **`\"seq\"` DSL**: `(build (lazy-seq (seq (items))))` — build applies with-meta when there's metadata; lazy-seq on cljd — meta nil ✓. Encode path: `seq?` → list-wire. But wait — `wire-of` checks `(or (list? x) (seq? x))` — LazySeq ✓.\n\nHmm, but `list-wire` docstring says \"(tag, [name items])\" items is d+3, deep! is (+ d 2). Frame is tag (d) → array (d+1) → items (d+2)? Let me re-check depth accounting: list frame = tag 27 over [name, items-array]. Items at depth d: tag d, its array content d+1, array's elements d+2. But code says deep! (+ d 2) and items are (+ d 3). Off by one? For identifier-wire: deep! (+ d 3), components are at d+3: tag d, array d+1, elements d+2. Code uses d+3 — more conservative than needed? Hmm, E4 says list frames consume 3 levels: tag(1), array(2), items(3). So the tag itself is d, array is d+1, items are d+2... \"consume 3 levels\" means items are at d+2 relative to frame's d? Nested-list test: k lists → CBOR depth 3k. For k=1: tag(d1), array(d2), inner empty array(d3)? \"dao.jing/list\" frame payload: [name-string, items-array]. Top tag depth 1; its content array depth 2; items inside that array depth 3. Yes — items at d+2 when frame is d... but code wires items at (+ d 3). Hmm: list-wire is called with x at depth d (tag is d). Payload array at d+1, items inside array at d+2. But code: `(deep! (+ d 2) ...)` then items are `(+ d 3)`. This counts one extra level. Wait — deep! (+ d 2) checks that d+2 ≤ 128 for... tag is d, array is d+1, so deep! checks array is d+1? They check +2 and items at +3. So one level conservative. But the test pins: nested-list 42 → depth 126 = 3×42 ✓, 43 → 129 rejected ✓. So the depth model matches E4 (3 per frame) — meaning per frame levels consumed = 3, so items are conceptually at d+3, where d is the *next* frame's tag... whatever — tests pin behavior and pass on all hosts; consistent since reader uses the same accounting? Reader: read-item increments d per tag/array. For a list frame decode: tag depth d, payload array d+1, items d+2. Hmm, so reader counts items at d+2 but encoder requires items at d+3?! If encoder is more conservative than decoder, encode at depth 128 might... Test: nested-list-hex 42 decodes fine (reader depth: 42 frames × 3 = 126? Let me compute reader depth for 42 nested lists: outer tag depth 1, its array depth 2, items: inner tag depth 3... per frame +3, innermost empty array: frame 42's array at depth 3×42-1=125? items array at 126? nested-list-hex 42 = 42 tags and arrays + innermost 80. Reader depth of innermost empty array: tag1=1, array1=2, tag2=3, array2=4, ..., tag42=83, array42=84? Wait that's 2 per frame + innermost item... Actually nested-list-hex k = (k-1)×\"d81b 82 <name> 81\" + \"d81b 82 <name> 80\". Per frame: tag(d), array(d+1), name-string(d+2), items-array(d+2). Next frame's tag at d+3. So k frames: innermost items-array at depth 3(k-1)+... = tag_k at 3(k-1)+1, array_k at 3(k-1)+2 = 3k-1. For k=42: 125 ≤ 128 ✓; k=43: 128... 3×43-1 = 128 ✓ accepted? But test says nested-list-hex 43 → :malformed-cbor. Hmm 3×43-1 = 128, deep! rejects when d > 128 → 128 not rejected. But test expects rejection at 43. Let me recount: frame i's tag at depth 3(i-1)+1. k=43: tag43 at 127, array43 at 128, name at 129 → rejected ✓ (name string item is at depth 129 > 128: read-item is called on array elements at inc d = 129 → deep! rejects). OK so reader rejects k=43 due to name at 129. And encoder: list-wire is d, items at d+3: frame i's items (inner list) at... inner list's own tag = d+3 where outer tag is d? items are wired at (+ d 3) — the inner value's outermost item (its tag) sits at d+3. So encoder also consumes 3 per frame ✓. And identifier's name is at d+3 ✓ consistent with reader (name at array d+1's element → d+2? Reader: tag d, array d+1, name d+2). Encoder checks (+ d 3), wires text at d+3 — encoder is one deeper than reader for identifier. Encoder more conservative than decoder would mean some value encodes to bytes that decode rejects? E4: \"both count same CBOR depths so anything encode outputs, decode accepts\". For identifier: encoder requires d+3 ≤ 128; produced bytes: tag(d), array(d+1), name(d+2) → reader needs d+2 ≤ 128. Encoder stricter → anything encoded decodes ✓. Not a soundness issue, just off-by-one conservatism; probably existed since J1 (identifier-wire not Dart specific). Actually is it a problem in the other direction: reader accepts a payload that encode would reject on re-encode? Decode requires re-encode match — if reader accepts an identifier at name depth 128 (tag at 126), re-encode checks tag at d+3 = 129 > 128 → rejects with :unsupported-value instead of returning the value! So there could exist a payload where decode succeeds structurally but fails re-encode with :unsupported-value rather than returning the value. Is that a contract violation? E4 says \"anything encode outputs, decode accepts\" — that direction is fine. The other direction (decode accepts things encode rejects) shouldn't happen for canonical payloads, since canonical payloads are by definition encodable. A payload with a deeply nested identifier at exactly that boundary that decode accepts but re-encode rejects — the rejection class would be :unsupported-value from encode inside decode... decode's contract is to accept exactly canonical payloads; such a payload at the edge of the depth limit — is it canonical? If encode never produces it (encoder rejects), it's not in the canonical set, and rejecting is correct, but the class :unsupported-value instead of :malformed-cbor for decode rejection is weird. Extremely edge-case, existing since J1 (identifier-wire unchanged?), not Dart specific. Could mention as P3. Actually wait — reader depth for identifier: tag at d (deep! d), read-item recurses into content at d+1 (array), array's elements at d+2 (name text, deep! d+2). So max tag depth for reader acceptance = 126. Encoder: identifier at tag depth d requires deep! (+ d 3) ≤ 128 → d ≤ 125. So tag at depth 126: reader accepts, re-encode rejects with :unsupported-value. Payload bytes would have name at depth 128 = allowed by reader. So decode of such a payload throws :unsupported-value. Contract: \"decode accepts exactly one canonical payload\" — canonical means what encode can produce, so rejecting is correct; class mismatch (:unsupported-value during decode, rather than :malformed-cbor) is minor. But is this new in J2? deep! (+ d 3) in identifier-wire — probably from J1. Not a regression; P3 at most. Same question for list-wire: deep! (+ d 2) and items at d+3. Reader: list frame tag d, array d+1, items at d+2, name also at d+2. So reader accepts items at d+2 where encoder puts at d+3? Encoder's deep! (+ d 2) checks... hmm, encoder's items-wire at (+ d 3) but deep! only (+ d 2). Reader for inner item at d+2 → accepts up to tag depth d=128 for inner? Inner tag at reader depth d+2 ≤ 128 → outer d ≤ 126. Encoder: outer frame at d, inner tag wired at d+3, its own deep! at its depth → d+3 ≤ 128 → d ≤ 125. Again off by one, same minor issue. Existing since J1. Test pins nested-list 42/43 passing. Fine — but 42×3=126, reader's innermost array at 125, name at... consistent.\n\nOK, these depth off-by-ones are existing J1 behavior, pinned by tests; not J2 findings beyond noting.\n\n23. **`encode` at depth: `wire-of value 1`** ✓.\n\n24. **`decimal` on Dart stores exponent as int (via .toInt) — decimal-exponent returns int; re-encode int-wire(big e) ✓.\n\n25. **Dart's `hash` function on strings/vecs** — cljd provides ✓.\n\n26. **`(= (count coll) expected)`** ✓.\n\n27. **`constructible?` uses tree-seq coll? seq** — on Dart, `not-constructible` is a keyword `::not-constructible` = :dao.jing.cbor-fixtures/not-constructible. `(some #(= not-constructible %) (tree-seq coll? seq value))` — fine for `host-value \"char\"` returning not-constructible as top-level value.\n\n28. **`host-value \"inst\"` on Dart: DateTime.parse** → encode rejects with :unsupported-value (type-name). wire-of falls to :else → rejected ✓. But `constructible?` — tree-seq over DateTime: coll? is false → checks only itself ✓.\n\n29. **`\"uuid\"` on Dart: `(uuid v)`** — cljd's uuid → rejected with :unsupported-value ✓.\n\n30. **`\"fn\"` identity** — rejected ✓.\n\n31. **`(->Probe 1)` record** — record? on cljd? Does cljd support defrecord? The fixture defines `(defrecord Probe [a])` at line 139 — does cljd compile this? If cljd doesn't support defrecord, this fixture wouldn't compile on Dart... but the lane passes, so it does (ClojureDart supports defrecord? I think cljd supports deftype/defrecord). And `record?` dispatch on cljd — does cljd implement record?? If record? is false for Probe on Dart, then Probe would fall through wire-of dispatch: not map?, not vector?... defrecord is a map → `(map? x)` true → would encode as a map! Unless record? catches first. wire-of checks `(record? x)` before `(map? x)` (lines 1013-1014) ✓. Does cljd have record?? If not, compilation would fail on unresolved symbol → lane wouldn't pass. Since it passes, record? exists and returns true for cljd records (does cljd's defrecord produce a type satisfying clj's record?? Hmm — actually in cljd, defrecord is... I can't verify; tests pass so `host/record` case rejects correctly on Dart. encode-rejection case with record input — is that rejected as :unsupported-value? If record? is false on Dart, map? is true → map-wire of {:a 1} → encodes fine → no rejection → test fails. Lane passes → fine.)\n\n32. **`refusal-of` on Dart catches `Object`** — Dart throws Exception/Error; Object catches all ✓. But note: catching Object in cljd... ex-info is an Exception ✓.\n\n33. **`guarded` catches Object** ✓.\n\n34. **`type-name` on Dart: `.-runtimeType`** ✓.\n\n35. **Now the question of \"Dart `is` failure throws\" in decode runner** — addressed above: not handled in canonical/decode runners; failures still surface but hide later cases. Also: inside `guarded`, the catch calls `(is false ...)` which throws on Dart → propagates → but importantly, doseq interruption means remaining cases don't run; the test as a whole fails (which is correct signal). P3.\n\nActually wait — is it true that `is` throws on Dart? In cljd's clojure.test port, assertion failures do... The implementer's own report says so and restructured one runner accordingly. If `is` throws, then in `refusal-of`... no `is` there. In canonical runner, `is` failures throw inside `testing` inside `guarded`'s f → caught by guarded → `(is false ...)` throws → doseq interrupted → deftest fails. Result: failure is reported (as a thrown exception with the message id \" threw ...\"). Later cases hidden. P3 (diagnostics only).\n\nBut also the final `(is (empty? @unexpected) ...)` in encode-rejection — if that `is` throws on Dart, deftest fails ✓ fine (last statement).\n\n36. **`skip!` uses println** — output on Dart is fine.\n\n37. **`groups-hold-on-produced-bytes`** — runs on Dart ✓ no `is` throw guard... uses is inside doseq; if it fails, it throws and hides rest. Same P3 class.\n\n38. **`encoding-ignores-ambient-print-settings`** — `binding [*print-length* ...]` on cljd — do these vars exist in cljd? cljd's clojure.core has *print-length* etc. binding works ✓ (lane passes).\n\n39. **`payloads-are-one-item-then-end`** — `(re-find #\"d90100|d819\" h)` — regex literal on cljd: does cljd support #\"...\"? ClojureDart supports regex literals (Dart RegExp). re-find on cljd — implemented ✓ (lane passes).\n\n40. **`jing/sha256-bytes`** on Dart — existing (J1? dao.jing probably has cljd half) ✓.\n\nNow **question 2 details**: \"does the package re-encode, reorder, narrow (integer double to int) or allocate differently?\"\n\n- Reordering: CborMap.fromIterables preserves insertion order per docs; adapter sorts. ✓ But `CborMap.fromIterables` — if the keys iterable is a clj vector — does it copy to a Dart map internally? CborMap stores LinkedHashMap of key→value; if two keys are `==` in Dart, they collapse! Keys are CborValue objects — CborValue implements ==? In cbor package, does CborValue subclass implement equality? If CborString(\"a\") == CborString(\"a\") (value equality), duplicate keys would collapse — but duplicates are rejected earlier by hex. Different hex → different bytes → are they == as objects? CborInt(1) vs CborSmallInt(1): hmm, would both be produced for the same integer by the shared code? `integer` chooses based on Dart type. Two keys \"1\" (int) and BigInt 1 (CborInt) — different bytes? CborSmallInt 1 → 0x01; CborInt BigInt.one → also 0x01! Same bytes → hex duplicate check rejects first ✓. So CborMap equality-based collapsing could only lose entries with identical bytes, which are rejected earlier. But wait — is CborInt == CborSmallInt with same value? In package, CborSmallInt extends CborInt; equality compares values → they could be ==. Irrelevant per above.\n\n  But another collapse risk: **CborMap.fromIterables building LinkedHashMap where key equality is by Dart == on CborValue objects.** Two keys with different bytes but equal by ==? E.g., CborString \"a\" vs CborString \"a\" — same bytes. CborBytes vs CborString — different types, not equal. CborInt vs CborFloat? Does the package have CborFloat? Only via tagged frame — float is encoded as a byte string in a frame, never CborFloat. So no.\n\n  Also **CborMap.fromIterables could throw on duplicate keys**? If it detects them? Doesn't matter.\n\n- Narrowing: package never receives a double except through byte strings in frames — float64 frame payload is bytes ✓. CborFloat not used ✓.\n\n- Re-encoding strings: CborString writes UTF-8 of the Dart string. Dart strings with unpaired surrogates write '?' — guarded by check-text before CborString creation. All paths to CborString? `text` (called from wire-of after check-text ✓, and identifier-wire after check-text ✓), and `frame` name (a compile-time symbol ✓). And `tagged`/others none. Decode path: utf8 strict decode → valid string, no lone surrogates possible (strict decoder rejects ED A0 80 etc) ✓. But wait — BOM restoration prepends U+FEFF — fine.\n\n  Hmm, actually one more path: **`String.fromCharCodes` in fixtures for {\"utf16\" codes}** — can create lone surrogate strings for rejection tests; codec rejects in check-text","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the contract, sign-off, work package, README, and corpus checks.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_fd69bce38a3f4424925b3175","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md"}}]}}
{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, read the contract document (docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md) — the \"Encoding contract\" and \"Numeric identity\" sections — and then also check the architect's J1 sign-off and the J2 section of the work package. Also, I need to verify the JVM/Node non-regression issue: I need the git diff of boring.cljc. Without shell access, I can't get the old version. However, the J1 committed version is probably in git objects; I can't read it. But I can reason: J1's cbor.cljc used to build the wire directly with boring values (e.g., `(into {} ...)`, vectors, TaggedValue). Currently it goes through adapter functions, which are identity on JVM/Node. Identity functions return exactly the same thing. The only concern: `bool` returns `b` — but in wire-of, `(wire/bool x)` where x is a boolean; identity. `null` returns nil. `text` returns s. `byte-string` returns bs. `integer` returns n. `array-node` returns nodes (a vector — previously mapv produced a vector; still mapv). `map-node` does `(into {} (map ...))` from wired triples — previously, map-wire probably built `(into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [kw vw]) ...))`. Question: does the key order of the map passed to boring affect anything? Boring's :canonical sorts keys, so it doesn't.\n\nOne subtle change: members-wire now computes `wire/encode` of each key node for hex-sorting purposes — this probably also existed in J1 (map keys are sorted by canonical byte order for duplicate detection). Actually, in J1's map, since Boring sorts the keys, the encode side only needed duplicate detection by canonical bytes. Same now. But wait — `map-node` on JVM/Node builds a Clojure map with key node → value node; duplicate detection is done before construction via `wired`'s hex. No problem.\n\nPotential regression path: `set-wire` sorts with `(sort-by first wired)`, where first is the hex string; the result is passed to `wire/tagged 258 (mapv second ...)`. Previously probably the same. `sort-by` on hex strings — stable sort, lexicographic ordering on lowercase hex: does lexicographic ordering on hex strings match byte-wise canonical ordering? In CBOR's canonical map key ordering (RFC 8949 core deterministic: byte-wise lexicographic on the encoded keys), shorter prefix comes first. Lowercase hex string comparison: \"0a\" vs \"0a01\": \"0a\" is a prefix and shorter → comes first. Byte-wise: [0x0a] vs [0x0a, 0x01] → shorter prefix first. Same. Hex characters: '0'-'9' < 'a'-'f' in ASCII, and byte value ordering matches nibble ordering. So no problem. This is also used in cljd.cljd's map-node.\n\nNow, main review points to verify by reading the code:\n\n1. **Correctness of Dart writer.**\n   - `integer`: uses CborSmallInt for Dart's int, CborInt for BigInt. Question: does `cbor` 6.5.1's CborSmallInt write the shortest head including 8-byte? CborSmallInt takes an `int`; for values beyond the signed 64-bit range... Dart's int is signed 64-bit. b->int on Dart: `(.toInt b)` if `isValidInt` — Dart's BigInt.isValidInt is true when it fits in a signed 64-bit int. So integers in [-2^63, 2^63-1] are Dart int; values in [2^63, 2^64-1] and [-2^64, -2^63-1] are BigInt → CborInt. Does CborInt handle negative BigInts? In cbor package 6.x, CborInt covers both major types 0 and 1, accepting BigInt. Probably fine. Can I read the package source if it's vendored? It's in the pub cache, not in the repo. Check if there's a pubspec/deps directory in the repo... The report says the package source is outside allowed directories. Can't verify package internals; I'll rely on the report's probe claims and note residual risks.\n   \n   - Actually wait: does `cborEncode` for CborSmallInt with a negative int write major type 1 correctly? Standard. Shortest head: the package should write minimal representation. The implementer probed it. But one subtle trap: CborSmallInt for values ≥ 2^63 can't exist as Dart int. Fine.\n   \n   - `text`: `CborString. s` — does the package write minimal UTF-8? CborString stores the string and encodes as UTF-8, major type 3, with length = UTF-8 byte length. Should. But: does the package write the string as-is, or re-encode surrogates? The codec checks surrogates first in check-text. But wait — where is check-text applied? In wire-of for strings, identifier-wire for ns/name. On decode, `utf8` strictly decodes; then re-encode. OK.\n   \n   - Bigger trap: CborString with `.type` default is definite. Yes.\n   \n   - `byte-string`: CborBytes over Uint8List — writes major type 2 with byte count. OK.\n   \n   - `tagged` for byte content: `CborBytes. content .tags [tag]` — writes the tag head then the byte string. Tags 2/3 with minimal magnitude bytes: magnitude-bytes returns a minimal big-endian Uint8List; for zero → empty list, tag 2 over empty byte string (0xc240) — canonical for bignum 0? Actually, zero in a corpus shouldn't use bignum (it's in 64-bit range). But re-encoding for decode: tag 2/3 payload comes from the wire, and the canonicality check requires re-encode to match; if input had non-minimal magnitude bytes, re-encode differs → rejected as non-canonical. Fine.\n   \n   - `frame`: tag 27 over CborList [CborString name, content]. OK.\n   \n   - `array-node`: CborList with .type definite. Does CborList accept a Clojure vector via `(vec nodes)`? In cljd, `vec` produces a CLJ vector implementing List? `(cbor/CborList. (vec nodes) ...)` — CborList's constructor expects a Dart List. ClojureDart vectors... hmm. ClojureDart's clj vector is a Dart object but does it implement Dart's `List` interface? I think ClojureDart collections implement Dart interfaces (clj vectors implement List). Actually yes: ClojureDart maps implement Dart Map and vectors implement List for interop. I recall cljd vectors satisfy `is List`. OK.\n   \n   - `map-node`: CborMap.fromIterables with Clojure vectors of keys and values (mapv). Fine. Keys sorted by hex. But duplicate keys impossible (checked earlier). Note: CborMap.fromIterables — does the package dedupe or reorder? Map keys here are cbor objects (CborString, CborInt, etc.). If two distinct wire nodes are `==` as Dart objects, fromIterables might collapse them? Keys are already duplicate-checked by hex, so distinct bytes; but could distinct nodes compare equal via Dart equality? E.g., CborInt 1 and CborSmallInt 1? Both would encode to the same bytes, so hex duplicate check catches them. What about CborString(\"a\") vs... same bytes → caught. So no.\n   \n   - `encode`: `Uint8List.fromList (cborEncode wire)` — does cborEncode return Uint8List? In cbor 6.x, `cborEncode(value)` returns `Uint8List`. Actually is it the cbor package's top-level function `cborEncode`? Package API: I think `cborEncode(CborValue value)` exists as `Uint8List cborEncode(CborValue value)` in cbor 6.x. Yes (added around 6.0 as a replacement for cbor.encode? there's a `cborEncode` function). OK.\n   \n   - One real question: **tag encoding with CborBytes `.tags [tag]`** — does the package write the tag head first? Yes, tags are written before the item.\n   \n   - **CborInt vs CborSmallInt**: in cbor 6.5.1, CborSmallInt is a subtype of CborInt for values that fit in int. `CborInt. n` where n is BigInt. Fine.\n   \n   - Potential trap: `CborString` writes text with length in bytes — but if the string contains non-BMP characters (surrogate pairs), UTF-8 encoding is 4 bytes; fine.\n\n2. **Shared reader Dart host traps:**\n   - `read-head` for ai 24-26: `(+ (* acc 256) (byte-at bs i))` — Dart int is 64-bit, max 4-byte arg is 2^32-1, fine.\n   - `byte-at` on Dart uses `(. bs \"[]\" i)`, Uint8List → returns int 0-255. Fine.\n   - ai 27 `span-big`: builds BigInt from bytes. Fine.\n   - `length-of`: rejects on Dart if BigInt exceeds max-length; `.toInt` OK. But note: on Dart, `host-big?` checks `dart/is? x BigInt`. read-head's `(< ai 26)` path returns a Dart int → not BigInt → fine.\n   - But: **Dart int overflow in span-key / bytes-hex?** None.\n   - `read-item` major type 7, ai 27 → `:float` item — then `item->value :float` rejects :native-float. Note that float items of ai 25/26 also get `:float` kind `(item :float ai pos)` — for ai 25/26/27, the kind is `:float` and value is ai... Wait, looking: `(= ai 24)` is simple; `:else` → `(item :float ai pos)`. ai ∈ {25,26,27} → :float. item->value rejects :native-float. Good — but ai 25/26 half/single floats: canonical rejection class should be :native-float per corpus. OK, no behavior change.\n   \n   - **BOM handling in Dart's `utf8` function**: the loop strips BOM sequences (0xef 0xbb 0xbf) and prepends U+FEFF characters. Condition `(<= (+ at 3) end)` — fine. Then decode the rest with Utf8Decoder. Is Utf8Decoder strict by default? dart:convert's Utf8Decoder: default constructor `Utf8Decoder({this.allowMalformed = false})` — strict, throws FormatException on overlong/surrogates/out-of-range. Good.\n   \n   Wait — is it true that Dart's utf8.decode strips the BOM? Actually yes: Dart's `Utf8Decoder` strips a leading BOM (documented behavior: \"decoding strips a leading byte-order mark\"). The fix restores it. But subtlety: the fix prepends `boms` U+FEFF characters — but does Dart's decoder strip only one BOM or multiple? Documented: strips a leading BOM (one). If input has two BOMs, decoder strips the first, leaves the second as content? Hmm — actually the decoder strips one leading BOM; the second BOM remains as U+FEFF content. The loop strips all leading BOMs and re-prepends all of them, and decoding starts after them, so it's correct either way.\n   \n   Another subtlety: **indefinite-length text chunks** (lines 1149-1177): each chunk is decoded with `utf8`; on Dart, a chunk beginning with BOM bytes gets BOM handling — correct. Chunks cannot split a BOM mid-sequence... A chunk could end with 0xef and the next start with 0xbb 0xbf — in that case, chunk 1's decoding of 0xef fails as invalid UTF-8 → rejected. That's the correct canonical behavior anyway (indefinite text is not in profile; profile only accepts definite? Actually, profile — does decode accept indefinite-length text at all? re-encode check: encode always writes definite, so indefinite input fails re-encode → non-canonical. But strict UTF-8 is also checked first. Fine.)\n   \n   - **`check-text` on Dart**: uses `.codeUnitAt` — Dart strings are UTF-16 code units, so unpaired surrogate detection works the same as JS. Good. But wait — `count` on a string in ClojureDart: does `(count s)` return the number of code units or runes? In ClojureDart, `count` on a String is... cljd's string implementation: `clj.core/count` on a string — I think ClojureDart treats strings as CharSequence with code-unit semantics (like Clojure JVM's .length? no — JVM's count is the number of UTF-16 code units via String.length). In ClojureDart, does String implement clj's ISeqable? Hmm. ClojureDart docs: strings are seqable by code unit? Recalling cljd's `count` on a String... In ClojureDart, `String` is given clj protocols; `seq` of a string yields single-character strings? Actually, in ClojureDart `seq` on a String yields chars? Dart has no char type. ClojureDart's `count` on a String returns length in code units (String.length). I think cljd defines count on strings as `.length` (code units), consistent with JVM. And `subs` also uses code-unit indices. If count returned rune count and char-code used code-unit indices, check-text could be inconsistent for astral characters. Risky to assert without checking cljd source. Since surrogate pairs are 2 code units and also counted as 2 (rune = 1 for pair). If count is rune-based, for \"a\\uD83D\\uDE00\", n would be 2 (rune count), and char-code at i=1 would be codeUnitAt(1) = 0xD83D high surrogate, check i+1=2 < n=2? no → rejected as unpaired surrogate — false rejection of valid text! That would be a P1 if count were rune-based. But the corpus probably has astral test cases (uni/*). The report says the canonical corpus passes on Dart — if the corpus has emoji/astral text cases and the full cljd lane passes, then count must be code-unit based. Corpus case \"uni/astral\" probably exists. Let me check corpus for astral cases. Also `subs`/`apply str` etc.\n   \n   Actually, ClojureDart: strings are represented as Dart Strings; cljd implements `-count` on String as `.length` (UTF-16 code units) — fairly confident since it aims for JVM semantics.\n   \n   - **`b->int`**: Dart's `.isValidInt` / `.toInt`. For values that fit in signed 64-bit → int. Encoding `integer` uses CborSmallInt for int. Note: Dart int is signed 64-bit; values in [-2^64, -2^63-1] are BigInt → CborInt. Good.\n   \n   - **`magnitude-bytes`** on Dart: loops over bytes, `(Uint8List.fromList (vec acc))` — acc is a list of ints built by prepending (conj to () list) — wait, `conj` to a Clojure list prepends. Loop: acc starts as (), each iteration prepends the low byte, then b >>= 8. So acc is little-endian → reversed? Let's trace: b = 0x0102. Iteration 1: prepend (b & 0xff)=0x02 → acc=(0x02); b=0x01. Iteration 2: prepend 0x01 → acc=(0x01 0x02). Result list order: (0x01, 0x02) — big-endian. Correct.\n   \n   - **`span-big`** uses `(big (byte-at bs i))` → on Dart `(big int)` → BigInt.from. Fine.\n   \n   - **`float-fields` on Dart**: `(>= hi 0x80000000)` — Dart's getUint32 returns int; comparison is fine. `(bit-shift-right hi 20)` — hi is non-negative (uint32), fine.\n   \n   - **`float-wire` on Dart**: ByteData big-endian by default (Endian.big is default). setFloat64 → getUint8List... `.asUint8List (.-buffer bd)` — full 8 bytes. NaN → canonical 7ff8000000000000. Good. But note: on Dart, does `setFloat64` for NaN preserve payload bits? For non-NaN, writes f directly; NaN case is handled. But: Dart VM canonicalizes NaN — `.-isNaN ^double f` then writes fixed bits. Good.\n   \n   - **Signed zero on Dart**: `(.-isNaN)` is false; setFloat64 of -0.0 writes 8000000000000000. Dart preserves -0.0 as double. And `float64?` on Dart: `dart/is? x double` — -0.0 is double,