p internally? CborMap stores LinkedHashMap of key→value; if two keys are `==` in Dart, they collapse! Keys are CborValue objects — CborValue implements ==? In cbor package, does CborValue subclass implement equality? If CborString(\"a\") == CborString(\"a\") (value equality), duplicate keys would collapse — but duplicates are rejected earlier by hex. Different hex → different bytes → are they == as objects? CborInt(1) vs CborSmallInt(1): hmm, would both be produced for the same integer by the shared code? `integer` chooses based on Dart type. Two keys \"1\" (int) and BigInt 1 (CborInt) — different bytes? CborSmallInt 1 → 0x01; CborInt BigInt.one → also 0x01! Same bytes → hex duplicate check rejects first ✓. So CborMap equality-based collapsing could only lose entries with identical bytes, which are rejected earlier. But wait — is CborInt == CborSmallInt with same value? In package, CborSmallInt extends CborInt; equality compares values → they could be ==. Irrelevant per above.\n\n  But another collapse risk: **CborMap.fromIterables building LinkedHashMap where key equality is by Dart == on CborValue objects.** Two keys with different bytes but equal by ==? E.g., CborString \"a\" vs CborString \"a\" — same bytes. CborBytes vs CborString — different types, not equal. CborInt vs CborFloat? Does the package have CborFloat? Only via tagged frame — float is encoded as a byte string in a frame, never CborFloat. So no.\n\n  Also **CborMap.fromIterables could throw on duplicate keys**? If it detects them? Doesn't matter.\n\n- Narrowing: package never receives a double except through byte strings in frames — float64 frame payload is bytes ✓. CborFloat not used ✓.\n\n- Re-encoding strings: CborString writes UTF-8 of the Dart string. Dart strings with unpaired surrogates write '?' — guarded by check-text before CborString creation. All paths to CborString? `text` (called from wire-of after check-text ✓, and identifier-wire after check-text ✓), and `frame` name (a compile-time symbol ✓). And `tagged`/others none. Decode path: utf8 strict decode → valid string, no lone surrogates possible (strict decoder rejects ED A0 80 etc) ✓. But wait — BOM restoration prepends U+FEFF — fine.\n\n  Hmm, actually one more path: **`String.fromCharCodes` in fixtures for {\"utf16\" codes}** — can create lone surrogate strings for rejection tests; codec rejects in check-text","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the contract, sign-off, work package, README, and corpus checks.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_fd69bce38a3f4424925b3175","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_566be619393e48bb846d62f3","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790027800000-ref-architect-j1-signoff.findings.md"}}]}}
