teger-width-duplicate` live on Dart? E1: JVM is N/A (merged) because JVM's `(= 1 1N)` is true so map keys merge. Dart: int 1 vs BigInt 1 — `=` is false → preserved → live, refused ✓ (the report says integer-width preserves both).\n\nHmm, but actually on Dart, the input DSL builds a map with entries keyed by int 1 and BigInt 1 via `(reduce (fn [m [k v]] (assoc m k v)) {} ...)` — cljd's assoc uses clj equality — different types → both preserved ✓.\n\nAnd `coll/set-signed-zero-collapse` merges on Dart: 0.0 vs -0.0 — clj equality on Dart doubles: `(= 0.0 -0.0)` is true (Dart semantics) → merges ✓ N/A.\n\n`coll/set-vector-list-collapse` merges: cljd's `(= [1] '(1))` is true (clj semantics: vector vs list equivalence is… hmm, in Clojure, `(= [1] '(1))` is TRUE (sequential equality across vector/list). Yes, clj's = treats vectors and lists as equal if items are equal. So a set with [1] and '(1) would merge on JVM/Node/Dart alike via clj equality → merged everywhere ✓ (E1 says N/A on both JVM and Node).\n\n`coll/set-decimal-scale-collapse`: Decimal(-3, 0) vs 0? Members are presumably decimal 0E-3 and decimal 0 or int 0. JVM merges (BigDecimal equality? JVM uses `.equals` for map keys? Clojure's = on BigDecimal uses compareTo → 0M = 0.000M is true → merge). Dart: Decimal carrier with -equiv comparing exponent and mantissa → different → preserved → live ✓. Node: carriers → live ✓ consistent with E1.\n\n`coll/set-nan-payload-duplicate` / `map-nan-payload-duplicate`: Node merges because Float64 carrier's -equiv treats NaNs as equal? Wait no, the carrier's -equiv: `(and (js/isNaN v) (js/isNaN other))` → NaNs are equal → merges into a JS set? Actually Node's set is built with `reduce conj #{}` using clj equality → carrier equality makes NaN=NaN true → merge → N/A. Dart: decoded floats are plain doubles; NaN in a cljd set — `(= NaN NaN)` in cljd is… Dart's double equality is NaN != NaN → preserved → live ✓. JVM: Double/NaN — does clj's = on Double use .equals? Clojure's numeric equality treats NaN = NaN as true (boxed comparison uses Double/compare? actually Clojure's `(= Double/NaN Double/NaN)` is true because Numbers.equal uses equals on boxed doubles). So JVM preserves both?? E1 says JVM is live, refused — meaning JVM's set preserves both NaN members. Hmm, if `(= NaN NaN)` is true in clj then `#{NaN NaN}` merges to one. But the fixture presumably uses float64 with different payloads — on JVM they're both Double NaN and… E1 says JVM live/refused and Node merged. Whatever — the Dart claim: two NaNs don't merge (Dart's `==` is false for NaN) ✓ consistent with the proposal.\n\n4. Next, check `num/js-unsafe-integer` in the README's Obligations to validate the skip's wording. Also check the \"Dart\" mention in the README.\n\nAlso let me validate the question about `is` failures in the decode-refusal runner, and check the remaining few subtle points in the code:\n\n**Subtle issue A: `refusal-of` catches `Object` on Dart — does it also catch skip!? No.\n\n**Subtle issue B: inside `guarded`, `(is false ...)` — if `is` throws on Dart, the exception from guarded propagates and aborts the doseq. The canonical and decode runners use `is` inside guarded — the first failure aborts the rest of the cases in that deftest. The report acknowledges this only for encode-refusal. This is a P3 diagnostic gap; it doesn't affect the verdict.\n\nActually wait — more subtle: does `is` really throw on Dart? In cljd's clojure.test, the `is` macro reports failures to `*report-counters*`/`do-report`, and cljd's test framework might implement failure reporting by throwing (cljd.test runs tests with zones, and `is` failures… hmm). The implementer observed it, so I'll trust that.\n\n**Subtle issue C: `skip!` uses println — no problem.\n\n**Subtle issue D: `collapse-not-applicable` — the encode-refusal runner only checks merges via `(and n (< (count v) n))` against the top-level DSL member count. For a set with signed-zero: `(reduce conj #{} items)` merges → count < n ✓.\n\nBut the NaN case on Dart: not in collapse-not-applicable, so it must not merge on Dart. If a future Dart/cljd version normalizes NaN equality… the test would fail loudly (MERGED is collected → the final is fails). ✓ fail-loudly design.\n\n**Subtle issue E: `wire/bool` inside identifier-wire? None.\n\n**Subtle issue F: in the `integer` adapter in cljd.cljd, when n is a Dart int, `CborSmallInt. n`. b->int produces a Dart int for values in [-2^63, 2^63-1] ✓. But int-wire calls `(wire/integer (b->int b))` only when b is in [-2^64, 2^64). For b in [2^63, 2^64-1], b->int returns BigInt → CborInt ✓.\n\n**Subtle issue G — `CborInt.` vs `CborSmallInt.`: in cbor 6.5.1, is there a `CborSmallInt` class? Let me recall the package API. The cbor package (by Yehuda Kremer? no — the `cbor` package by \"Cbor Dart\"… the maintainer is…). cbor 6.x API: `CborValue`, `CborNull`, `CborBool`, `CborString`, `CborBytes`, `CborList`, `CborMap`, `CborInt`, `CborSmallInt`, `CborFloat`, `CborDecimal`… yes, cbor 6.x has CborSmallInt (subtype of CborInt for values that fit in int). CborSmallInt's constructor takes an int. ✓. And CborInt's constructor takes a BigInt. ✓.\n\nHmm wait — actually in cbor 6.x, `CborInt`'s constructor: `CborInt(BigInt value)`. And `CborSmallInt(int value)`. Correct.\n\ncborEncode: in cbor 6.x is it `Uint8List cborEncode(CborValue value)`? There's a `cborEncode` top-level function. ✓.\n\n**Subtle issue H — tag number encoding: does CborBytes with `.tags [2]` write `0xc2` + the byte-string head? Tag 2 → 0xc2 ✓. Tag 27 → 0xd8 0x1b ✓. Tag 258 → 0xd9 0x01 0x02 ✓. Tag 30 → 0xd8 0x1e ✓. Tag 4 → 0xc4 ✓. The package writes shortest tag heads ✓ (probed/corpus).\n\n**Subtle issue I — `CborString` length: text \"hello\" → 0x65 head. For longer strings, 8-byte heads (rare). No issue.\n\n**Subtle issue J — `frame`: `(str tag-symbol)`. In cljd, `str` on a symbol gives the name with ns ✓. But could cljd's symbol str escape anything? 'dao.jing/float64 → \"dao.jing/float64\" ✓. 'clojure/