e = tag 27 over [name, items-array]. Items at depth d: tag d, its array content d+1, array's elements d+2. But code says deep! (+ d 2) and items are (+ d 3). Off by one? For identifier-wire: deep! (+ d 3), components are at d+3: tag d, array d+1, elements d+2. Code uses d+3 — more conservative than needed? Hmm, E4 says list frames consume 3 levels: tag(1), array(2), items(3). So the tag itself is d, array is d+1, items are d+2... \"consume 3 levels\" means items are at d+2 relative to frame's d? Nested-list test: k lists → CBOR depth 3k. For k=1: tag(d1), array(d2), inner empty array(d3)? \"dao.jing/list\" frame payload: [name-string, items-array]. Top tag depth 1; its content array depth 2; items inside that array depth 3. Yes — items at d+2 when frame is d... but code wires items at (+ d 3). Hmm: list-wire is called with x at depth d (tag is d). Payload array at d+1, items inside array at d+2. But code: `(deep! (+ d 2) ...)` then items are `(+ d 3)`. This counts one extra level. Wait — deep! (+ d 2) checks that d+2 ≤ 128 for... tag is d, array is d+1, so deep! checks array is d+1? They check +2 and items at +3. So one level conservative. But the test pins: nested-list 42 → depth 126 = 3×42 ✓, 43 → 129 rejected ✓. So the depth model matches E4 (3 per frame) — meaning per frame levels consumed = 3, so items are conceptually at d+3, where d is the *next* frame's tag... whatever — tests pin behavior and pass on all hosts; consistent since reader uses the same accounting? Reader: read-item increments d per tag/array. For a list frame decode: tag depth d, payload array d+1, items d+2. Hmm, so reader counts items at d+2 but encoder requires items at d+3?! If encoder is more conservative than decoder, encode at depth 128 might... Test: nested-list-hex 42 decodes fine (reader depth: 42 frames × 3 = 126? Let me compute reader depth for 42 nested lists: outer tag depth 1, its array depth 2, items: inner tag depth 3... per frame +3, innermost empty array: frame 42's array at depth 3×42-1=125? items array at 126? nested-list-hex 42 = 42 tags and arrays + innermost 80. Reader depth of innermost empty array: tag1=1, array1=2, tag2=3, array2=4, ..., tag42=83, array42=84? Wait that's 2 per frame + innermost item... Actually nested-list-hex k = (k-1)×\"d81b 82 <name> 81\" + \"d81b 82 <name> 80\". Per frame: tag(d), array(d+1), name-string(d+2), items-array(d+2). Next frame's tag at d+3. So k frames: innermost items-array at depth 3(k-1)+... = tag_k at 3(k-1)+1, array_k at 3(k-1)+2 = 3k-1. For k=42: 125 ≤ 128 ✓; k=43: 128... 3×43-1 = 128 ✓ accepted? But test says nested-list-hex 43 → :malformed-cbor. Hmm 3×43-1 = 128, deep! rejects when d > 128 → 128 not rejected. But test expects rejection at 43. Let me recount: frame i's tag at depth 3(i-1)+1. k=43: tag43 at 127, array43 at 128, name at 129 → rejected ✓ (name string item is at depth 129 > 128: read-item is called on array elements at inc d = 129 → deep! rejects). OK so reader rejects k=43 due to name at 129. And encoder: list-wire is d, items at d+3: frame i's items (inner list) at... inner list's own tag = d+3 where outer tag is d? items are wired at (+ d 3) — the inner value's outermost item (its tag) sits at d+3. So encoder also consumes 3 per frame ✓. And identifier's name is at d+3 ✓ consistent with reader (name at array d+1's element → d+2? Reader: tag d, array d+1, name d+2). Encoder checks (+ d 3), wires text at d+3 — encoder is one deeper than reader for identifier. Encoder more conservative than decoder would mean some value encodes to bytes that decode rejects? E4: \"both count same CBOR depths so anything encode outputs, decode accepts\". For identifier: encoder requires d+3 ≤ 128; produced bytes: tag(d), array(d+1), name(d+2) → reader needs d+2 ≤ 128. Encoder stricter → anything encoded decodes ✓. Not a soundness issue, just off-by-one conservatism; probably existed since J1 (identifier-wire not Dart specific). Actually is it a problem in the other direction: reader accepts a payload that encode would reject on re-encode? Decode requires re-encode match — if reader accepts an identifier at name depth 128 (tag at 126), re-encode checks tag at d+3 = 129 > 128 → rejects with :unsupported-value instead of returning the value! So there could exist a payload where decode succeeds structurally but fails re-encode with :unsupported-value rather than returning the value. Is that a contract violation? E4 says \"anything encode outputs, decode accepts\" — that direction is fine. The other direction (decode accepts things encode rejects) shouldn't happen for canonical payloads, since canonical payloads are by definition encodable. A payload with a deeply nested identifier at exactly that boundary that decode accepts but re-encode rejects — the rejection class would be :unsupported-value from encode inside decode... decode's contract is to accept exactly canonical payloads; such a payload at the edge of the depth limit — is it canonical? If encode never produces it (encoder rejects), it's not in the canonical set, and rejecting is correct, but the class :unsupported-value instead of :malformed-cbor for decode rejection is weird. Extremely edge-case, existing since J1 (identifier-wire unchanged?), not Dart specific. Could mention as P3. Actually wait — reader depth for identifier: tag at d (deep! d), read-item recurses into content at d+1 (array), array's elements at d+2 (name text, deep! d+2). So max tag depth for reader acceptance = 126. Encoder: identifier at tag depth d requires deep! (+ d 3) ≤ 128 → d ≤ 125. So tag at depth 126: reader accepts, re-encode rejects with :unsupported-value. Payload bytes would have name at depth 128 = allowed by reader. So decode of such a payload throws :unsupported-value. Contract: \"decode accepts exactly one canonical payload\" — canonical means what encode can produce, so rejecting is correct; class mismatch (:unsupported-value during decode, rather than :malformed-cbor) is minor. But is this new in J2? deep! (+ d 3) in identifier-wire — probably from J1. Not a regression; P3 at most. Same question for list-wire: deep! (+ d 2) and items at d+3. Reader: list frame tag d, array d+1, items at d+2, name also at d+2. So reader accepts items at d+2 where encoder puts at d+3? Encoder's deep! (+ d 2) checks... hmm, encoder's items-wire at (+ d 3) but deep! only (+ d 2). Reader for inner item at d+2 → accepts up to tag depth d=128 for inner? Inner tag at reader depth d+2 ≤ 128 → outer d ≤ 126. Encoder: outer frame at d, inner tag wired at d+3, its own deep! at its depth → d+3 ≤ 128 → d ≤ 125. Again off by one, same minor issue. Existing since J1. Test pins nested-list 42/43 passing. Fine — but 42×3=126, reader's innermost array at 125, name at... consistent.\n\nOK, these depth off-by-ones are existing J1 behavior, pinned by tests; not J2 findings beyond noting.\n\n23. **`encode` at depth: `wire-of value 1`** ✓.\n\n24. **`decimal` on Dart stores exponent as int (via .toInt) — decimal-exponent returns int; re-encode int-wire(big e) ✓.\n\n25. **Dart's `hash` function on strings/vecs** — cljd provides ✓.\n\n26. **`(= (count coll) expected)`** ✓.\n\n27. **`constructible?` uses tree-seq coll? seq** — on Dart, `not-constructible` is a keyword `::not-constructible` = :dao.jing.cbor-fixtures/not-constructible. `(some #(= not-constructible %) (tree-seq coll? seq value))` — fine for `host-value \"char\"` returning not-constructible as top-level value.\n\n28. **`host-value \"inst\"` on Dart: DateTime.parse** → encode rejects with :unsupported-value (type-name). wire-of falls to :else → rejected ✓. But `constructible?` — tree-seq over DateTime: coll? is false → checks only itself ✓.\n\n29. **`\"uuid\"` on Dart: `(uuid v)`** — cljd's uuid → rejected with :unsupported-value ✓.\n\n30. **`\"fn\"` identity** — rejected ✓.\n\n31. **`(->Probe 1)` record** — record? on cljd? Does cljd support defrecord? The fixture defines `(defrecord Probe [a])` at line 139 — does cljd compile this? If cljd doesn't support defrecord, this fixture wouldn't compile on Dart... but the lane passes, so it does (ClojureDart supports defrecord? I think cljd supports deftype/defrecord). And `record?` dispatch on cljd — does cljd implement record?? If record? is false for Probe on Dart, then Probe would fall through wire-of dispatch: not map?, not vector?... defrecord is a map → `(map? x)` true → would encode as a map! Unless record? catches first. wire-of checks `(record? x)` before `(map? x)` (lines 1013-1014) ✓. Does cljd have record?? If not, compilation would fail on unresolved symbol → lane wouldn't pass. Since it passes, record? exists and returns true for cljd records (does cljd's defrecord produce a type satisfying clj's record?? Hmm — actually in cljd, defrecord is... I can't verify; tests pass so `host/record` case rejects correctly on Dart. encode-rejection case with record input — is that rejected as :unsupported-value? If record? is false on Dart, map? is true → map-wire of {:a 1} → encodes fine → no rejection → test fails. Lane passes → fine.)\n\n32. **`refusal-of` on Dart catches `Object`** — Dart throws Exception/Error; Object catches all ✓. But note: catching Object in cljd... ex-info is an Exception ✓.\n\n33. **`guarded` catches Object** ✓.\n\n34. **`type-name` on Dart: `.-runtimeType`** ✓.\n\n35. **Now the question of \"Dart `is` failure throws\" in decode runner** — addressed above: not handled in canonical/decode runners; failures still surface but hide later cases. Also: inside `guarded`, the catch calls `(is false ...)` which throws on Dart → propagates → but importantly, doseq interruption means remaining cases don't run; the test as a whole fails (which is correct signal). P3.\n\nActually wait — is it true that `is` throws on Dart? In cljd's clojure.test port, assertion failures do... The implementer's own report says so and restructured one runner accordingly. If `is` throws, then in `refusal-of`... no `is` there. In canonical runner, `is` failures throw inside `testing` inside `guarded`'s f → caught by guarded → `(is false ...)` throws → doseq interrupted → deftest fails. Result: failure is reported (as a thrown exception with the message id \" threw ...\"). Later cases hidden. P3 (diagnostics only).\n\nBut also the final `(is (empty? @unexpected) ...)` in encode-rejection — if that `is` throws on Dart, deftest fails ✓ fine (last statement).\n\n36. **`skip!` uses println** — output on Dart is fine.\n\n37. **`groups-hold-on-produced-bytes`** — runs on Dart ✓ no `is` throw guard... uses is inside doseq; if it fails, it throws and hides rest. Same P3 class.\n\n38. **`encoding-ignores-ambient-print-settings`** — `binding [*print-length* ...]` on cljd — do these vars exist in cljd? cljd's clojure.core has *print-length* etc. binding works ✓ (lane passes).\n\n39. **`payloads-are-one-item-then-end`** — `(re-find #\"d90100|d819\" h)` — regex literal on cljd: does cljd support #\"...\"? ClojureDart supports regex literals (Dart RegExp). re-find on cljd — implemented ✓ (lane passes).\n\n40. **`jing/sha256-bytes`** on Dart — existing (J1? dao.jing probably has cljd half) ✓.\n\nNow **question 2 details**: \"does the package re-encode, reorder, narrow (integer double to int) or allocate differently?\"\n\n- Reordering: CborMap.fromIterables preserves insertion order per docs; adapter sorts. ✓ But `CborMap.fromIterables` — if the keys iterable is a clj vector — does it copy to a Dart map internally? CborMap stores LinkedHashMap of key→value; if two keys are `==` in Dart, they collapse! Keys are CborValue objects — CborValue implements ==? In cbor package, does CborValue subclass implement equality? If CborString(\"a\") == CborString(\"a\") (value equality), duplicate keys would collapse — but duplicates are rejected earlier by hex. Different hex → different bytes → are they == as objects? CborInt(1) vs CborSmallInt(1): hmm, would both be produced for the same integer by the shared code? `integer` chooses based on Dart type. Two keys \"1\" (int) and BigInt 1 (CborInt) — different bytes? CborSmallInt 1 → 0x01; CborInt BigInt.one → also 0x01! Same bytes → hex duplicate check rejects first ✓. So CborMap equality-based collapsing could only lose entries with identical bytes, which are rejected earlier. But wait — is CborInt == CborSmallInt with same value? In package, CborSmallInt extends CborInt; equality compares values → they could be ==. Irrelevant per above.\n\n  But another collapse risk: **CborMap.fromIterables building LinkedHashMap where key equality is by Dart == on CborValue objects.** Two keys with different bytes but equal by ==? E.g., CborString \"a\" vs CborString \"a\" — same bytes. CborBytes vs CborString — different types, not equal. CborInt vs CborFloat? Does the package have CborFloat? Only via tagged frame — float is encoded as a byte string in a frame, never CborFloat. So no.\n\n  Also **CborMap.fromIterables could throw on duplicate keys**? If it detects them? Doesn't matter.\n\n- Narrowing: package never receives a double except through byte strings in frames — float64 frame payload is bytes ✓. CborFloat not used ✓.\n\n- Re-encoding strings: CborString writes UTF-8 of the Dart string. Dart strings with unpaired surrogates write '?' — guarded by check-text before CborString creation. All paths to CborString? `text` (called from wire-of after check-text ✓, and identifier-wire after check-text ✓), and `frame` name (a compile-time symbol ✓). And `tagged`/others none. Decode path: utf8 strict decode → valid string, no lone surrogates possible (strict decoder rejects ED A0 80 etc) ✓. But wait — BOM restoration prepends U+FEFF — fine.\n\n  Hmm, actually one more path: **`String.fromCharCodes` in fixtures for {\"utf16\" codes}** — can create lone surrogate strings for rejection tests; codec rejects in check-text","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the contract, sign-off, work package, README, and corpus checks.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_fd69bce38a3f4424925b3175","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_566be619393e48bb846d62f3","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790027800000-ref-architect-j1-signoff.findings.md"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790033129381,"messageId":"4a36a8c8-1293-4fa6-962a-e54dc98eae3f"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_fd69bce38a3f4424925b3175","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 564 lines\n1: # DaoJing: Backend-Independent CBOR Storage\n2: \n3: Status: implementation plan; not yet implemented. Architecture-reviewed\n4: 2026-09-17 (`collab/1789680000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor.*`): no\n5: blocking findings, one medium-severity ambiguity (file-backend frame\n6: ownership, corrected below), and one cross-cutting change (the\n7: `dao.space.index`/`query` comparator changes) flagged as needing explicit\n8: sign-off from `dao.space`'s owners before this plan is built.\n9: \n10: Related: [DaoJing](dao.jing.md), [DaoJing DHT](dao.jing.dht.md),\n11: [architectural foundations](datom.world.md).\n12: \n13: ## Objective and invariant\n14: \n15: DaoJing is a backend-independent storage substrate, like Datomic storage.\n16: It can store content in memory, a file, PostgreSQL, S3, or another backend.\n17: **This invariant must be preserved. CBOR is the stored data format, not a\n18: choice of storage backend.**\n19: \n20: Replace Jing's handwritten hash encoding and EDN persistence with canonical\n21: CBOR bytes. Jing owns the value-to-bytes contract, content addressing, and\n22: integrity verification. Backends store and retrieve opaque bytes by address\n23: and provide their own durability guarantees. They need no knowledge of CBOR,\n24: Clojure values, datoms, indexes, or manifests. **One exception, narrower\n25: than \"backend\" vs. \"not a backend\":** `dao.jing.file` is the only backend\n26: that re-ingests its own output across process death — it must persist an\n27: append-only log and later replay it with no external message boundary to\n28: mark records, so it alone must invent a self-describing frame, which is\n29: why that frame is itself a CBOR structure (a two-element\n30: `[digest payload-bytes]` array; see *Memory and files*). `dao.jing.mem`\n31: never persists, so it has no framing at all; `dao.jing.remote`/`dao.jing.dht`\n32: get record boundaries for free from Transit's own message envelopes, so\n33: they frame with Base64-inside-Transit, never a raw CBOR array. This is not\n34: about `dao.jing.file` being more or less \"Jing's own\" than any other\n35: backend in this source tree — all of `memory`/`file`/`remote`/`dht` are\n36: equally in-repo; only PostgreSQL and S3 are genuinely third-party and not\n37: yet built. What is third-party-implementable is the byte-store *contract*\n38: itself, not any particular shipped implementation of it. The exception is\n39: that `dao.jing.file` parses its own frame with Jing's shared codec, never a\n40: divergent per-backend codec or value interpretation.\n41: \n42: ```text\n43: write: value -> canonical CBOR bytes -> SHA-256 address -> backend\n44: read:  address -> backend bytes -> integrity verification -> decoded value\n45: \n46: backend: memory | file | PostgreSQL | S3 | remote | DHT | ...\n47: ```\n48: \n49: PostgreSQL and S3 illustrate the required interchangeability; implementing\n50: new PostgreSQL or S3 backends is outside this codec migration.\n51: \n52: ## Community precedent and integration choice\n53: \n54: Reuse [Boring](https://github.com/replikativ/boring), the CBOR serializer built\n55: for Datahike. Konserve integrates it as a pluggable JVM/JavaScript serializer\n56: (id 3), but marks it BETA, \"Not yet recommended for production stores\";\n57: Fressian remains its default (verified against\n58: [Konserve serializers](https://github.com/replikativ/konserve/blob/main/src/konserve/serializers.cljc),\n59: 2026-09-16).\n60: Jing will reuse serialization directly without adopting Konserve's storage\n61: API, mutable document operations, or Datahike-specific type handlers.\n62: \n63: Pin `org.replikativ/boring` to `0.1.30` on JVM and JavaScript, verified as the\n64: latest release on 2026-09-16 against\n65: [Clojars](https://clojars.org/org.replikativ/boring). Implement the same\n66: Jing-supported representation on Dart. Boring's upstream\n67: [compatibility policy](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/doc/COMPATIBILITY.md)\n68: preserves released tag meanings and older-output readability, but does not\n69: promise identical encoding bytes across releases (verified against that\n70: policy, 2026-09-16). Jing's pin and frozen byte/hash fixtures own byte\n71: stability; Konserve's integration does not. Dependency upgrades must pass\n72: those fixtures without regeneration.\n73: \n74: Select `:canonical` and explicitly set encode options for stringref off,\n75: shapes off, and no index frame. Verified against the\n76: [Boring README](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/README.md),\n77: 2026-09-16: `:clojure` defaults to string deduplication and preserved float\n78: width, `:canonical` uses bytewise key ordering and shortest numeric forms,\n79: and `:canonical-rfc7049` uses length-first ordering. Option precedence over\n80: profile defaults is unverified; fixtures must prove the effective options,\n81: rather than relying on profile selection alone.\n82: \n83: ## Layering and interfaces\n84: \n85: - Introduce `dao.jing.cbor/encode` and `decode`, operating on host byte\n86:   arrays. Share normalization and validation across hosts; isolate Dart's\n87:   codec implementation behind the same interface.\n88: - Preserve the public value-facing `materialize!`, `get`, `segment-key`,\n89:   `content-hash`, and `close!` operations. Existing callers still write and\n90:   read values.\n91: - Introduce an explicit byte-store boundary below those operations:\n92:   `{:put-bytes-fn f :get-bytes-fn g :close-fn c}`. Put takes an address and\n93:   canonical payload bytes and returns `:inserted` or `:present`; get takes\n94:   an address and caller-supplied not-found sentinel and returns bytes or\n95:   that sentinel. A shared Jing wrapper exposes the existing content-store\n96:   handle over this byte store.\n97: - This contract is effect-payload-shaped (address, bytes → verdict), so it\n98:   can become the deferred effect-stream write path in `dao.jing.md` without\n99:   a contract break; the synchronous function handle is its present-day\n100:   shape, not a commitment. The put half is effect-shaped as stated; the get\n101:   half becomes effect-shaped once its outcome is the explicit\n102:   `{:found? boolean, :bytes b}` envelope — `dao.jing.remote` already\n103:   answers the `{:found? boolean, :value v}` shape this generalizes\n104:   (`remote.cljc:55-60,84-85`; `:bytes` is this plan's future key, not\n105:   `:value`, since the value here is a byte representation, not a decoded\n106:   Clojure value) — with bytes in a portable byte representation — the\n107:   caller-supplied not-found sentinel is a wrapper-level convenience over\n108:   that envelope, not part of the contract's streamable shape.\n109: - Encode once before a write, derive the address from those bytes, and pass\n110:   those same bytes to the backend. On `:present`, read back and verify the\n111:   bytes by hash and byte-for-byte equality with the proposed payload. Never\n112:   overwrite conflicting content. Return an address only after\n113:   the backend reports its applicable storage guarantee.\n114: - The wrapper derives and verifies addresses; a backend re-verifies a put\n115:   exactly when bytes arrive from an untrusted party, such as a remote/DHT\n116:   server checking a client's claim.\n117: - Verify every retrieved payload against its requested address before\n118:   decoding exactly one supported value. Canonicality verification (decode,\n119:   re-encode, byte-compare) occurs exactly once at each ingress of bytes Jing\n120:   did not encode itself: remote/DHT receipt and file replay acceptance,\n121:   before storage, caching, or exposure. Ordinary reads of accepted snapshots\n122:   hash-verify and decode without re-encoding. Validation belongs to the\n123:   shared codec/wrapper at those boundaries, not to backend value handlers;\n124:   immutable local forwarding does not repeat ingress validation. Missing\n125:   content remains distinct from stored nil, which has a CBOR representation.\n126: - Store immutable snapshots. Memory backends copy byte arrays on insertion\n127:   and retrieval so caller mutation cannot change previously addressed\n128:   content. Other backends must provide the same observable isolation.\n129: - Keep backend handles and composition explicit. Introduce no global\n130:   backend registry, ambient codec options, or domain-specific interpretation.\n131: \n132: ## Encoding contract\n133: \n134: Use Boring's `:canonical` profile with string references, shaped arrays, and\n135: offset indexes disabled. Jing's normalization and named CBOR extensions are\n136: fixed, not backend-configurable. The hash covers only canonical payload\n137: bytes, never a backend coordinate, file frame, address envelope, or network\n138: message.\n139: \n140: Supported values are nil, booleans, strings, keywords, symbols, byte strings,\n141: vectors, finite lists/sequences, maps, sets, metadata, integers,\n142: floating-point numbers, big integers, decimals, and ratios. Reject arbitrary\n143: records, functions, and unsupported host objects before storage. The numeric\n144: carriers described below are explicitly supported exceptions to the record\n145: restriction; arbitrary Boring records do not become Jing values. **This is\n146: a narrower domain than today's transitional `pr-str`-based encoder, which\n147: addresses anything printable/readable** — characters, `#inst`, `#uuid`, and\n148: other tagged literals are addressable today and have no slot in this\n149: supported-values list or in Jing's four named CBOR extensions; they become\n150: rejected, unsupported values once this plan lands. No current producer\n151: emits them, so nothing observed breaks, but \"existing stores... must be\n152: rebuilt together\" (*Addressing and clean break*) presumes every stored\n153: value is re-encodable under the new domain — an assumption this delta\n154: should be checked against before rebuild, not discovered during it.\n155: \n156: - Normalize sorted collections to ordinary maps and sets. Sort map keys\n157:   and set elements lexicographically by unsigned canonical encoded bytes\n158:   (a proper prefix sorts first), not length-first. Reject duplicate canonical\n159:   keys or elements introduced by normalization instead of losing entries.\n160:   Supported maps/sets must also survive portable decoded equality without\n161:   collapsing entries: reject such collisions on encode and before decoded\n162:   collection construction, including numerically equal keys of different\n163:   kinds. Do not let the accepting host determine this boundary.\n164: - Encode strings as UTF-8 without Unicode normalization. Reject unpaired\n165:   high or low surrogates before encoding on every host, including in\n166:   identifier components and metadata; normal astral text and noncharacters\n167:   remain legal. Reject invalid UTF-8 on decode. Boring's own surrogate\n168:   pre-validation is unverified; Jing owns this rule regardless.\n169: - Lists and sequences share a representation distinct from vectors. Use\n170:   CBOR tag 27 with the name `dao.jing/list` and an array argument; plain\n171:   CBOR arrays represent vectors.\n172: - Preserve collection and symbol metadata using Boring's `clojure/with-meta`\n173:   mapping. Strip `:line`, `:column`, `:end-line`, and `:end-column`; omit empty\n174:   metadata. Clear metadata on internally constructed Dart collections\n175:   before attaching supplied metadata. Do not strip arbitrary user `:tag`\n176:   metadata as a workaround for Dart constructor metadata. This clearing\n177:   covers only collections Jing constructs at decode: a Dart producer\n178:   building a payload with `list` must still clear ClojureDart's constructor\n179:   metadata itself (`(with-meta (apply list xs) nil)`); that `dao.jing.md`\n180:   open item carries forward, it is not retired by this plan.\n181: - Encode **all** keywords and symbols as tag 27 with `dao.jing/keyword` or\n182:   `dao.jing/symbol` and `[namespace name]`, where namespace is nil or a\n183:   string and name is a string, taken directly from the identifier's fields.\n184:   Uniform escaping removes a host-sensitive ordinary/escaped predicate.\n185:   Never parse or print these components to reconstruct an identifier;\n186:   preserve nil versus empty namespace and literal slashes, whitespace, and\n187:   leading colons. Verified against\n188:   [Boring's writer](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/src/boring/writer.cljs),\n189:   2026-09-16: its native tag 39 carries slash-joined text with a keyword\n190:   colon prefix, so distinct namespace/name pairs or colon-leading symbols\n191:   can collide. Jing has no native-identifier arm: reject tag 39, including\n192:   otherwise ordinary names, and enforce that same boundary by canonical\n193:   re-encoding at ingress.\n194: - Hash byte strings by content, independent of host object identity.\n195: - Reject malformed CBOR, unknown tags or named wrappers outside this\n196:   profile, duplicate map keys or set elements, trailing data, and\n197:   unsupported decoded values. Do not enable lossy encoder fallbacks.\n198: \n199: Jing's four names use tag 27 followed by exactly `[name-string, payload]`:\n200: `dao.jing/list` takes an array, `dao.jing/keyword` and `dao.jing/symbol` take\n201: the two-component arrays above, and `dao.jing/float64` takes an eight-byte\n202: byte string. Tag 27's object meaning is verified against the\n203: [IANA registry](https://www.iana.org/assignments/cbor-tags/cbor-tags.xhtml);\n204: the array-payload grammar and public mechanisms are verified against\n205: [Boring extension documentation](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/doc/EXTENDING.md)\n206: and [records.cljc](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/src/boring/records.cljc),\n207: 2026-09-16. Write these frames with `tagged-literal`; read them as\n208: `boring.data/UnknownRecord`, then convert recursively through a closed Jing\n209: name/shape dispatch before exposing values, preserving validated metadata\n210: when converting a wrapper to its value. Use no ambient registry or\n211: record/map constructor macros for these array payloads. Other UnknownRecords\n212: are errors. This chosen post-decode path deliberately does not set\n213: `:on-unknown-record :error`, which would reject Jing's own names; that\n214: option alone would not close numeric tags anyway.\n215: \n216: Explicitly reject inert `boring.data/TaggedValue` and every `SimpleValue`\n217: outside Jing's nil/boolean domain: unknown numeric tags survive Boring\n218: decoding as TaggedValue (verified against\n219: [data.cljc](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/src/boring/data.cljc),\n220: 2026-09-16). Validate tag/name/payload shapes before any lossy host\n221: conversion; successful generic decoding is not profile acceptance. Native\n222: CBOR floating-point items are forbidden even if a host decoder could turn\n223: one into an integer. Frozen fixtures define the remaining Boring mappings\n224: for sets and `clojure/with-meta`, whose payload is `[meta value]`; no other\n225: Boring collection or record mappings are admitted. All lengths and integer\n226: arguments use shortest definite encodings; integers fitting CBOR major\n227: types 0/1 do not use bignum tags, and bignum magnitudes have no leading zero.\n228: \n229: ### Numeric identity\n230: \n231: Preserve integer, floating-point, decimal, and rational kinds. This is an\n232: explicit user choice: an integer `1` and floating-point `1.0` have different\n233: addresses. Host integer width is not identity; small and big integers of the\n234: same value share an address.\n235: \n236: Carriers must implement portable `=`/`hash`/`compare` consistently across\n237: hosts, including carrier-to-native-number comparisons in both operand\n238: orders: compare numerically, never as disjoint record types. Finite values\n239: compare by exact mathematical value (including a float's exact binary\n240: value), without rounding through double; equal numeric values compare zero\n241: and hash equally, independent of kind, decimal scale, and zero sign.\n242: Order negative infinity below finite values, positive infinity above them,\n243: and canonical NaN last; canonical NaNs are equal to each other for this\n244: portable contract. Content identity remains stricter than numeric equality:\n245: integer/float kind, decimal scale, and float zero sign still affect bytes.\n246: \n247: **This is the only place this plan threads new runtime behavior into an\n248: existing consumer's comparison/equality logic outside `dao.jing*` — a real\n249: boundary widening, not a violation.** (The Implementation sequence's step 5\n250: also touches `dao.data.btree.md` §5.2's verification default, but that is\n251: a default flip §5.2 already pre-authorizes for exactly this trigger, not\n252: new or changed code; it carries none of this section's new-runtime-\n253: dependency weight and doesn't need the same sign-off.) Pushing portable\n254: `=`/`hash`/`compare` into `dao.space.index`'s\n255: datom comparators and `dao.space.query`'s builtins is architecturally\n256: sound (an interpreter consuming a storage-adjacent utility, not the\n257: reverse), but it is the widest blast radius in this plan and needs\n258: explicit sign-off from whoever owns `dao.space` before implementation\n259: starts on this section, separate from Jing's own review.\n260: \n261: These operations must govern datom ordering in `dao.space.index` —\n262: `compare-vals` and the EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET comparators; the generic\n263: `dao.data.btree` comparator needs no change — and `dao.space.query`\n264: matching/Datalog unification, including hashed lookup. `compare-vals`'s\n265: numeric arm must use the portable compare directly, with no string-ordering\n266: fallback for numbers. Portable `=` and `hash` recurse through collections,\n267: so a datom value containing a carrier unifies structurally. In\n268: `dao.space.query`, the comparison builtins (`= not= < > <= >= min max`)\n269: route through these portable operations; the arithmetic builtins\n270: (`+ - * / quot rem mod inc dec abs`) remain host-native and reject carrier\n271: operands loudly rather than computing garbage — queries that combine\n272: arithmetic builtins with floating, decimal, or rational datom values are\n273: therefore not portable across hosts until arithmetic itself is made\n274: portable, a recorded loud limitation consistent with VM arithmetic\n275: remaining outside this migration. Where host-native dispatch cannot satisfy\n276: the contract, the consumer's numeric comparison/equality/hash boundary must\n277: use explicit portable operations consistently. Reusing an upstream record\n278: representation alone does not satisfy this requirement. One intended\n279: consequence: covered-index membership and matching are by numeric value\n280: while content addressing is kind-strict, so `[e a 1 t m]`\n281: and `[e a 1.0 t m]` are one index entry with two distinct content\n282: addresses. **This is only half \"uniform with today's JVM behavior.\"**\n283: Covered-index membership already is (`compare-vals` already dispatches to\n284: host `compare`, and JVM `(compare 1 1.0)` is `0`). Query matching is not:\n285: `dao.space.query`'s `=` builtin binds host Clojure `=` directly\n286: (`query.cljc:751`), and JVM `(= 1 1.0)` is `false` today — Clojure's `=`\n287: is kind-strict. Routing `=` through the portable numeric-value equality\n288: above flips that to `true` on the JVM. This is a real, intended change to\n289: JVM query semantics, not a continuation of existing behavior, and the\n290: `dao.space` sign-off in the paragraph above should be read with that in\n291: mind. Also left open by this document: `min`/`max` over mixed numeric\n292: kinds that compare equal return one of the two operands, and which one is\n293: host-arbitrary today (Clojure's `min`/`max` return the second argument on\n294: a tie, e.g. `(min 1 1.0)` ⟹ `1.0`); this plan does not pin a tie-break\n295: rule for the portable versions, and it should before implementation.\n296: \n297: - Provide a portable `float64` constructor/carrier. JavaScript callers use\n298:   it for integral floating-point values such as `1.0`; ordinary integral\n299:   JavaScript numbers remain integers, except native negative zero, which\n300:   is floating-point content so its sign survives. Decoding floating-point content on\n301:   JavaScript returns the carrier so re-encoding cannot lose its kind.\n302: - Encode floating-point content on every host as tag 27,\n303:   `dao.jing/float64`, with eight big-endian IEEE-754 bytes. This avoids\n304:   Boring's native-number differences between JVM and JavaScript, verified\n305:   against its compatibility policy above: integral JS numbers encode as\n306:   integers while JVM `1.0` remains a float under `:canonical`.\n307:   Widen native float32 values to float64, preserve signed zero and\n308:   infinities, and canonicalize NaNs to quiet NaN bits `0x7ff8000000000000`.\n309:   **The Jing profile emits no native CBOR floating-point whatsoever**;\n310:   Boring's canonical float narrowing and NaN encoding never govern Jing\n311:   floating-point bytes. The carrier is Jing-owned; upstream has no float64\n312:   wrapper (verified against Boring `data.cljc`, 2026-09-16).\n313: - Use CBOR integers and bignum tags 2/3 for exact integers. Never convert\n314:   large integers through a floating-point intermediate. Reject unsafe\n315:   integral JavaScript Number inputs; callers must supply BigInt for exact\n316:   integers or the float64 carrier for floating-point content.\n317: - Use decimal-fraction tag 4 with `[exponent mantissa]`; preserve decimal\n318:   scale. Use rational tag 30 with a reduced numerator and positive,\n319:   nonzero denominator. Retain rational kind even when the denominator is 1.\n320:   Tags 2/3, 4, and 30 are registered meanings, verified against IANA above,\n321:   2026-09-16; Jing allocates no custom numeric tag.\n322: - Use native exact numeric types where they preserve the declared kind and\n323:   representation; otherwise use immutable carriers. Reuse Boring's\n324:   JavaScript decimal/rational representations, verified against Boring\n325:   `data.cljc`, 2026-09-16: `Decimal [exponent mantissa]` and\n326:   `Rational [numerator denominator]` exist on JVM and CLJS, with Rational\n327:   re-encoding as tag 30. Wrap/adapt them as needed for the portable numeric\n328:   operations above. Dart uses BigInt and explicit\n329:   decimal/rational carriers. Add portable constructors and accessors;\n330:   extending VM arithmetic is outside this migration.\n331: \n332: ## Backend changes\n333: \n334: All existing backends move to the shared byte-store boundary. No backend\n335: selects a different value codec or re-hashes a host-specific representation\n336: — with one named exception, `dao.jing.file`'s own frame parsing (see the\n337: Objective's carve-out, and *Memory and files* below).\n338: \n339: ### Memory and files\n340: \n341: The memory backend stores byte snapshots under content addresses. The file\n342: backend retains its append-only length framing, durability rules, duplicate\n343: validation, and torn-tail recovery. Each frame contains a CBOR\n344: `[digest payload-bytes]` record: digest is a raw 32-byte SHA-256 byte string\n345: and payload-bytes contains the exact canonical payload. **`dao.jing.file`\n346: itself owns parsing and constructing this two-element frame**, with Jing's\n347: own shared codec — the exception the Objective names above, needed because\n348: `dao.jing.file`'s frame shape is itself a CBOR structure, unlike\n349: `dao.jing.mem`'s unframed map or `dao.jing.remote`/`dao.jing.dht`'s\n350: Base64-inside-Transit framing. The wrapper owns keyword↔digest\n351: conversion, so file framing has no identifier-codec\n352: dependency. Replay checks the digest length and compares it byte-for-byte\n353: with the payload's SHA-256. The outer record is backend framing and does\n354: not contribute to the content hash. It is exactly one definite two-element\n355: CBOR array of byte strings, with shortest lengths and no trailing data,\n356: inside the existing four-byte big-endian signed length prefix; reject a\n357: frame exceeding that prefix's positive range before append.\n358: \n359: The byte-string payload is intentional: a file backend delegates payload\n360: canonicality acceptance at replay to the shared Jing validator, then stores\n361: and serves the original opaque bytes. It never replaces them with a decoded\n362: and re-encoded value. Duplicate digests require identical payload bytes.\n363: PostgreSQL byte columns and S3 objects obey the same ingress rule when opened\n364: over externally supplied bytes.\n365: \n366: ### Remote and DHT\n367: \n368: Carry canonical payload bytes as padded standard-alphabet Base64 strings\n369: (no whitespace or URL-safe alphabet) inside the existing Transit\n370: operation envelopes. Encode and decode this transport representation at\n371: Jing's network boundaries; leave general DaoStream Transit unchanged.\n372: Remote and DHT APIs above the byte-store wrapper remain value-facing.\n373: \n374: Decode Base64 strictly, hash-verify received bytes against the claimed\n375: address, and perform the one ingress canonicality check before accepting or\n376: caching them. Preserve explicit presence indicators, idempotent writes,\n377: correlated remote errors, and DHT refusal/next-candidate behavior. A found\n378: nil travels as Base64 of CBOR nil; an absent envelope keeps its explicit\n379: false indicator and nil placeholder, which is not Base64-decoded.\n380: \n381: The only current Jing application-level message byte cap is the DHT UDP\n382: budget of 1200 bytes (verified against `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc`,\n383: `max-datagram`); the WebSocket path enforces no byte cap today (verified\n384: against `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc`). For n payload bytes, Base64 occupies\n385: `4 × ceil(n / 3)` bytes, roughly 33% overhead. With E bytes of actual\n386: Transit envelope overhead, require `E + 4 × ceil(n / 3) ≤ 1200`, giving at\n387: most `3 × floor((1200 - E) / 4)` payload bytes; E varies by request/reply\n388: and coordinates, so check the final UTF-8 message size, not a fixed raw\n389: payload allowance. Oversized sends are refused and oversized replies\n390: dropped; DHT writes still acknowledge the local durable insert and degrade\n391: to local-only storage, while remote fetch attempts time out. Decide to keep\n392: WebSocket without a Jing byte cap in this migration: arbitrary payload\n393: length is limited by host resources, not a new codec-domain limit. This\n394: adds no UDP fragmentation or general-purpose transport protocol.\n395: \n396: The clean break requires coordinated peer upgrades; no version negotiation\n397: exists. A new-client put to an old server fails as an address mismatch\n398: (the old server hashes the Base64 string as a value); an old-client put to\n399: a new server fails at Base64 decoding for ordinary legacy values. A legacy\n400: string that happens to be valid Base64 must still pass CBOR profile and\n401: address verification and is rejected there, never silently reinterpreted.\n402: These failures remain loud remote errors or explicit DHT refusal; there is\n403: no legacy fallback.\n404: \n405: ### Other backends\n406: \n407: A PostgreSQL implementation may store address-to-byte-column entries; an S3\n408: implementation may store address-to-object-body entries. Their insertion,\n409: concurrency, and durability mechanisms belong to the backend. The byte-store\n410: contract requires that an existing address is never silently overwritten\n411: with different bytes. Neither backend needs CBOR value handlers.\n412: \n413: ## Addressing and clean break\n414: \n415: Hash canonical payload bytes directly with SHA-256. Preserve the address\n416: shape `:segment/sha256-<64 lowercase hex>`. Keep the existing string-based\n417: `sha256` helper for its other callers and add a separate byte-hashing function.\n418: \n419: Every newly encoded value receives its CBOR-derived address. No legacy\n420: reader, old-address alias, or graph migration is included. Reject old\n421: complete EDN file records without rewriting them. Existing stores and\n422: published references must be rebuilt together, including address-bearing\n423: indexes, ASTs, and continuations. **This states the rebuild's precondition\n424: explicitly: with no legacy reader, the only reconstruction path is\n425: replaying original values from their intake streams, not reading them back\n426: out of a rejected old store.** A value whose intake stream has since\n427: evicted it (a `:dao.stream/gap`) and whose old store is rejected under this\n428: plan has no remaining source — that content is unrecoverable, not merely\n429: inconvenient to rebuild. Rebuild readiness should be checked against\n430: retained intake history before this migration lands, not assumed. Existing\n431: torn-tail recovery applies only\n432: after the file is recognized as a valid new-format log; an old-format file\n433: must not be treated as an empty or recoverable new store.\n434: An empty file is a new log. Recognize a nonempty log only after its first\n435: complete frame passes the new frame, digest, and payload checks. Validate\n436: all complete frames before truncating an incomplete tail; any corrupt\n437: complete frame or a nonempty file with no valid first frame fails without\n438: mutation. This deliberately refuses an interrupted first write that cannot\n439: be distinguished from foreign content without adding a format header. The\n440: remedy for an interrupted first write is to delete or recreate the file: the\n441: interrupted put was never acknowledged, so no acknowledged content is lost\n442: and recreation is always safe.\n443: \n444: Update the main Jing design, backend documentation, and downstream canonical\n445: encoding references to describe the shared byte-store boundary and remove\n446: the superseded transitional-printer limitations. Name exactly which\n447: `dao.jing.md` open items this plan retires — the canonical-encoding item\n448: with its three residuals (symbol metadata not address-significant,\n449: pathological print collisions, ambient print-var leakage), byte-array\n450: identity hashing, and metadata carriage for the file/remote/DHT codecs —\n451: and which stay open: the intake transport's portable domain still carries\n452: no metadata, byte strings, or rich numerics (`dao.stream` Transit), so\n453: such payloads reach Jing only through direct `materialize!` calls and the\n454: intake fail-closed rule carries forward unchanged. The ClojureDart `list`\n455: producer obligation likewise carries forward, as *Encoding contract*\n456: records.\n457: \n458: ## Implementation sequence and validation\n459: \n460: 1. Write encoding-contract tests and frozen CBOR hex/SHA-256 fixtures before\n461:    implementation. Pin Boring and implement the JVM/JavaScript codec wrapper,\n462:    normalization, named extensions, and numeric constructors.\n463: 2. Implement the matching Dart codec and carriers, either hand-rolled or\n464:    over a Dart CBOR package, behind the shared interface — frozen fixtures\n465:    own conformance either way. Require all three hosts\n466:    to read each other's fixtures and reproduce identical canonical bytes.\n467: 3. Add byte hashing and the common value-facing wrapper; migrate memory and\n468:    file backends to opaque bytes. Preserve public value-facing call sites.\n469:    `dao.jing.cbor` exports the portable `=`/`hash`/`compare`; the numeric\n470:    arm of `dao.space.index/compare-vals` (the EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET datom\n471:    comparators) and `dao.space.query`'s comparison builtins route through\n472:    them, with the arithmetic builtins left host-native per *Numeric\n473:    identity* — the deepest of this migration's changes outside the\n474:    `dao.jing*` namespaces, threading new runtime behavior into an existing\n475:    consumer's comparison/equality logic (step 5's `dao.data.btree.md` §5.2\n476:    change is shallower: a pre-authorized default flip, not new code or a\n477:    new runtime dependency).\n478: 4. Migrate remote and DHT byte transport, including validation, missing-value\n479:    behavior, transport limits, and errors.\n480: 5. Update documentation and downstream address fixtures — including\n481:    `dao.data.btree.md` §5.2, whose same-host-only verification default is\n482:    **a default flip, not a code or format change**: §5.2 already\n483:    pre-authorizes this exact trigger (\"when the canonical byte encoding\n484:    lands... the default flips to on and the same-host restriction\n485:    disappears — the check itself needs no format change, only a stable\n486:    encoding under it\"). This CBOR migration is that stable encoding\n487:    landing; `dao.data.btree.storage`'s rehash-and-compare check is\n488:    unchanged code, now verified safe to default on. Then run the\n489:    repository's JVM, Node, and Dart suites and applicable lint checks.\n490: \n491: Required test scenarios:\n492: \n493: - Map/set insertion order; mixed-type keys; sorted collections; list/vector\n494:   distinction; collection and symbol metadata; empty maps, sets, vectors,\n495:   and lists with no metadata versus empty or reader-position-only metadata;\n496:   independence from ambient print settings.\n497: - Unicode astral text and noncharacters; no normalization of composed versus\n498:   decomposed text; reject `\"a\\uDC80b\"` and an unpaired high surrogate before\n499:   storage on JVM, Node, and Dart, including in metadata and identifiers.\n500: - Pathological identifiers: `(symbol \"42\")` versus integer 42;\n501:   `(keyword \"a/b\")` with ns nil/name \"a/b\" versus `(keyword \"a\" \"b\")`;\n502:   the corresponding symbol pair; names containing whitespace or a leading\n503:   colon; nil versus empty namespace. Construct exact component pairs when a\n504:   host's one-argument constructor parses slash text. All identifiers use\n505:   named frames; reject tag 39 even for ordinary names.\n506: - Frozen read/write fixtures for each of `dao.jing/list`,\n507:   `dao.jing/keyword`, `dao.jing/symbol`, and `dao.jing/float64`, including\n508:   malformed payload shapes; closed UnknownRecord conversion and effective\n509:   stringref/shapes/index disabling.\n510: - Byte-array content identity and mutation isolation before and after reads.\n511: - Integer versus integral float; signed zero; infinities and NaNs; integer\n512:   boundaries and bignums; decimal scale; reduced ratios and denominator 1.\n513: - Native float32 widening: a host float32 0.1 encodes the same eight bytes\n514:   as its exact float64 widening, distinct from float64 0.1; Node and Dart\n515:   reproduce the widened fixture, including signed-zero widening.\n516: - Node/Dart decoded numeric carriers in index-ordered datom positions\n517:   (`dao.space.index/compare-vals`, no string fallback) and\n518:   `dao.space.query` matched positions: native/carrier operands both ways,\n519:   mixed kinds, exact large values, hash lookup, equality and ordering,\n520:   signed zeros, infinities, NaN, and preserved kind on re-encoding. Also:\n521:   collection values containing carriers unify structurally; the comparison\n522:   builtins (`= not= < > <= >= min max`) over decoded carriers; the\n523:   arithmetic builtins over carriers reject loudly; and `[e a 1 t m]`\n524:   versus `[e a 1.0 t m]` collapse to one covered-index entry while keeping\n525:   two distinct content addresses.\n526: - Malformed and noncanonical encodings; duplicate keys; unknown tags;\n527:   duplicate set elements and decoded equality collapse; TaggedValue and\n528:   unsupported SimpleValue; native CBOR floats; invalid UTF-8; trailing data;\n529:   unsupported host values; address mismatches. Hash-valid noncanonical\n530:   payloads fail at ingress; repeated local hydration hash-verifies every\n531:   time without repeating canonical re-encoding.\n532: - File reopen, duplicate insertion, durability failures, torn tails,\n533:   complete corrupt frames, raw digest length/mismatch, and nondestructive\n534:   rejection of old EDN stores (including those with torn tails) and\n535:   unrecognizable first frames.\n536: - Memory/file/remote/DHT agreement on addresses and decoded content, including\n537:   metadata, binary values, rich numbers, stored nil, and missing content.\n538: - Network corruption, malformed Base64, mixed-version peers in both\n539:   directions (including Base64-shaped legacy strings), and peer failure.\n540:   DHT final-envelope sizes at and beyond 1200 bytes for puts and replies,\n541:   local-only oversized writes, and a WebSocket round trip beyond that\n542:   budget with no new Jing cap.\n543: - Existing Jing, published-index, AST-addressing, and continuation tests.\n544: - An independent CBOR reader inspecting fixtures, with explicit handlers for\n545:   Jing's named extensions and the correct deterministic ordering rule.\n546: \n547: Acceptance requires identical payload bytes and addresses across hosts,\n548: stable encode-decode-encode results for all supported values, and the same\n549: content address regardless of the selected storage backend. It also requires\n550: **injectivity**: pairwise-distinct supported content identities, including\n551: the pathological identifier class, must have pairwise-distinct encodings and\n552: fixture addresses. Identity here is after the explicitly declared\n553: normalizations (integer width, sortedness, sequence realization, stripped or\n554: empty metadata, reduced ratios, NaN payloads, and float32 widening); it\n555: retains numeric kind,\n556: decimal scale, signed zero, and retained metadata. SHA-256 collision\n557: resistance is the addressing assumption, not a mathematical injectivity\n558: claim about a finite digest. Acceptance includes every scenario above,\n559: specifically surrogate rejection, float32 widening, empty-metadata omission,\n560: carrier equality/hash/ordering and query matching, and identifier separation.\n561: No implementation is accepted on byte fixtures alone while these consumer\n562: semantics fail. Memory-mapped navigation, compression, arbitrary record\n563: handlers, new storage backends,\n564: and a redesign of Jing's write-effect protocol remain outside this work."}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_566be619393e48bb846d62f3","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 41 lines\n1: Completed-GMT: 2026-09-21 22:34:36 GMT  \n2: Completed-Local: 2026-09-22 05:34:36 Indochina Time  \n3: Coding-Agent: codex  \n4: Session-ID: 01a0c501-5311-71f0-94e5-033950e0473d\n5: \n6: J1 SIGNED OFF.\n7: \n8: 1. ADOPT — Decode must be Jing-owned structural validation followed by Boring re-encode verification; this is required by the plan’s “validate shapes before any lossy host conversion” rule and avoids Boring’s demonstrated losses. Boring remains the sole JVM/Node byte writer.\n9: \n10: 2. ADOPT — Ratify `host-collapse` as an additive 17th refusal class for host materialization failures involving joined-name keyword or symbol collisions. It does not change the Jing acceptance boundary: J3 must require identical outcomes across hosts except this explicitly named capability refusal, with JVM/Dart behavior determined by their actual equality models.\n11: \n12: 3. ADOPT — Ratify decimal exponents `[-2147483647, 2147483648]` and `max-depth 128`. These are host-independent safety rules: decode overflow is `malformed-cbor`; encode overflow is `unsupported-value`. They must be added to the design document’s Encoding contract before J2/step-3 integration.\n13: \n14: 4. ADOPT — The E1 corrected N/A table is authoritative; it reflects observed host construction and refusal behavior and does not alter any frozen bytes.\n15: \n16: 5. ADOPT — The fixed canonical Boring profile, ordinary non-indexed encoder, explicit outer `clojure/with-meta` frame, and unchanged existing entry points are sufficient. No further functional change is required before commit; the additive errata must be marked ratified.\n17: \n18: 6. ADOPT — J2 must mirror the structural-reader-before-package-decoder design, exact decimal window, depth limit, host-collapse policy, native-float rejection, decoded-float carrier preservation, and exact-integer safety. Host hash values must never be compared across hosts; fixture bytes and SHA-256 are the cross-host authority.\n19: \n20: Exact errata heading and opening wording:\n21: \n22: ```text\n23: Status: RATIFIED J1 ERRATA (architect sign-off 2026-09-22)\n24: \n25: This additive errata is ratified for J1 and J2. The frozen corpus remains\n26: unchanged; these entries define codec policy, host-capability refusals, and\n27: the corrected per-host test obligations.\n28: ```\n29: \n30: J2 constraints:\n31: \n32: - Use `cbor` 6.5.1 as the Dart byte writer; do not replace it with a hand-rolled parser.\n33: - Perform Jing-owned structural validation before package materialization.\n34: - Re-encode every decoded accepted value and require byte-for-byte equality.\n35: - Emit the outer `clojure/with-meta` frame explicitly, including around `dao.jing/list` and `dao.jing/symbol`.\n36: - Enforce the decimal exponent window and depth 128 identically.\n37: - Preserve float64 kind, signed zero, infinities, and canonical NaN; never narrow an integral float to an integer.\n38: - Use `BigInt` or explicit carriers for exact integers, decimal, and rational values; reject unsafe or lossy conversions.\n39: - Test Dart identifier equality for slash-crossed keyword/symbol components and report `host-collapse` only if Dart cannot materialize a frozen value without merging entries.\n40: - Treat fixture hex and SHA-256 as the interoperability contract; do not compare JVM, Node, or Dart hash-number results.\n41: - Maintain the additive boundary: no existing Jing behavior, backend, `dao.space`, remote/DHT, or transport changes."}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"01369769-3c01-4cda-abda-efdba9a44eb0"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, let's check the J2 section of the work package, the obligations in the corpus README, and verify specific corpus cases. Also, let's check coverage of astral text, BOM cases, int64 boundaries, keywords with slash separators, and metadata within the corpus JSON. The corpus is large, so I'll search for specific IDs with grep.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the work package J2 section and corpus README, plus targeted corpus checks.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_ec44a545424c463299bdf620","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"J2","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md","output_mode":"content","-A":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_4d5c2de408594046a68b14cb","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"\"id\": \"(uni/|num/int|coll/map-both|meta/|coll/set-signed|coll/set-vector-list|coll/map-integer-width|num/js-unsafe|host/)","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json","output_mode":"content"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790033140932,"messageId":"35e76d1d-d9a6-4db2-91bd-6285e83646d3"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_ec44a545424c463299bdf620","content":[{"type":"text","text":"collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md:29:The only cross-phase production edit should be to a namespace created by this unit: J1 creates the shared `dao.jing.cbor` facade, and J2 fills in its `:cljd` implementation route. No pre-existing source file needs modification.\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-30-\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-31-Neither owner gate blocks this unit. The first prohibited crossing is step 3, when the new codec becomes the address source, backends change to opaque bytes, and portable numeric semantics enter `dao.space`.\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-32-\ncollab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-33-One plan correction must be incorporated in the new implementation: Boring 0.1.30 does not reliably expose array-payload tag-27 frames as `boring.data/UnknownRecord`; that carrier is associated with record-shaped/map payloads. The wrapper must use `:on-unknown-record` to create a private tagged sentinel and then perform closed Jing name-and-shape dispatch. This stays within the new codec namespace and does not require an existing-code edit.\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-34-\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-35-This first unit can prove codec-level requirements, including portable numeric operations directly. It cannot claim the whole epic’s acceptance yet. Backend isolation, address-mismatch handling, file migration, remote/DHT agreement, and actual `dao.space` consumer behavior belong to steps 3–5.\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-36-\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-37-## 2. Bounded work package as phases\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-38-\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-39-A J0–J3 split is preferable to combining fixtures and implementation. It preserves the plan’s requirement that the contract be frozen before implementation and gives reviewers small, causally clear diffs.\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-40-\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-41-### J0 — Freeze the encoding contract\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-42-\ncollab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-43-Proposed file box:\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-44-\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-45-- New `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json`\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-46-- New `test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures.cljc`\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-47-- New fixture provenance document beside the resource, for example `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.README.md`\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-48-- No production source changes\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-49-- No dependency changes\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-50-\ncollab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-51-The JSON should contain only stable, language-neutral data: case ID, semantic input DSL, expected canonical hex, expected SHA-256, identity/equivalence group, and explanatory notes. JVM/Node can use the already-pinned `clojure.data.json`; Dart can use `dart:convert`.\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-52-\ncollab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-53-Completion criteria:\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-54-\ncollab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-55-- The corpus covers:\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-56-  - Map/set insertion order, mixed keys, sorted collections, and duplicate canonical/equality collapse.\ncollab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-57-  - List/vector distinction and all four tag-27 names: `dao.jing/list`, `dao.jing/keyword`, `dao.jing/symbol`, and `dao.jing/float64`.\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md-58-  - Metadata retention, reader-position stripping, empty-metadata omission, and symbol metadata.\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.g